<%!--
  MeetingLive — meeting shell + stage-mode switcher + embedded chat panel
  (ADR 0042).

  Meetings UI inherits the shell theme (`data-theme` is set on `<html>`
  by the layout in `root.html.heex`). All chrome tokens are semantic
  DaisyUI (`bg-base-100`, `text-base-content`, `border-base-300`).
  Video-tile gradients stay dark inside the tiles — those represent
  video feeds, not chrome.

  THIS task (019e2474-bc7c) ships:
    * the active stage-mode segmented control (top bar)
    * the four center-stage renderers: Canvas / Screen / Gallery /
      Whiteboard (`data-stage-mode` attribute drives renderer selection
      and is the e2e/test contract — keep it stable).

  Right pane embeds the canonical `PlatformWeb.ChatLive` surface in
  its `:embedded` mode (stage 5 of 019e2d1a; replaced the earlier
  `MeetingChatPanelLive` stub from sibling 019e2475-87a8). The mobile
  drawer toggle + intent-panel-host migration marker are preserved on
  the wrapping `<aside>`/`<div>`; only the embedded LV's identity
  changed.

  PLACEHOLDER slots for sibling stack members:
    * left rail        → 019e2475-4395 (sectioned participant list)
    * bottom strip     → 019e2475-0037 (video tiles)
--%>
<%!--
  Top-level `cond` (task 019f498d stage 4):
    * `@chrome_mode == :none and @state == :expired` — guest expired-invite card.
    * `@chrome_mode == :none and @state == :invalid` — guest invalid-invite card.
    * `@chrome_mode == :none and @state == :name_entry` — guest name-entry card.
    * `@meeting != nil` — full meeting shell (both `chrome_mode == :shell` and
      guest `:in_meeting`; shell-only regions gated with `@shell?` below).
    * Fallthrough — `:index` action (grouped meetings list + Start-meeting FAB).
  Guest sub-cards are rendered inline (with the outer `min-h-dvh` wrapper
  supplied by the `:guest` layout in `layouts.ex`). Stage 5 consolidated
  the guest surface onto MeetingLive; there is no longer a separate
  guest LV to keep in sync.
--%>
<%= cond do %>
  <% @chrome_mode == :none and @state == :expired -> %>
    <div class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-md flex-1 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-4 px-4">
      <div class="card w-full bg-base-200 shadow-lg">
        <div class="card-body items-center text-center">
          <span class="hero-clock size-10 text-warning" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h1 class="card-title text-lg">This invite has expired</h1>
          <p class="text-sm text-base-content/70">
            Guest invite links are only valid for a short window. Please ask the meeting host to send you a fresh link.
          </p>
          <div class="card-actions mt-2">
            <a href="https://example.com" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">
              Go to example.com
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% @chrome_mode == :none and @state == :invalid -> %>
    <div class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-md flex-1 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-4 px-4">
      <div class="card w-full bg-base-200 shadow-lg">
        <div class="card-body items-center text-center">
          <span class="hero-exclamation-triangle size-10 text-error" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h1 class="card-title text-lg">Invite link is not valid</h1>
          <p class="text-sm text-base-content/70">
            We couldn't verify this invite. It may have been mistyped, revoked, or refers to a meeting that no longer exists.
          </p>
          <div class="card-actions mt-2">
            <a href="https://example.com" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">
              Go to example.com
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% @chrome_mode == :none and @state == :name_entry -> %>
    <div class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-md flex-1 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-4 px-4">
      <div class="card w-full bg-base-200 shadow-lg">
        <div class="card-body">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="hero-video-camera size-6 text-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h1 class="card-title text-lg">Join {@meeting.name}</h1>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-base-content/70">
            Enter the name others in the meeting will see.
          </p>
          <form phx-submit="submit_name" class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-3">
            <label class="form-control w-full">
              <span class="label-text mb-1 text-sm">Your name</span>
              <input
                type="text"
                name="guest[name]"
                value={@name_value}
                autofocus
                required
                maxlength="80"
                placeholder="Jane Doe"
                class={[
                  "input input-bordered w-full",
                  @name_error && "input-error"
                ]}
              />
              <span :if={@name_error} class="mt-1 text-xs text-error">
                {@name_error}
              </span>
            </label>
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary w-full">
              Join meeting
            </button>
          </form>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% @meeting != nil -> %>
    <div class="flex h-full flex-col bg-base-100 text-base-content">
      <%!-- LiveKit JS hooks. MeetingClient listens for `join-meeting` /
         `leave-meeting` / `toggle-{mic,camera,screen-share}` push_events
         from MeetingHooks (server-side) and drives the local WebRTC
         session against `window.__livekit_room`. MeetingRoom subscribes
         to remote-track events so `<.video_tile>` `MeetingVideoTile`
         hooks can attach published tracks by identity. Both are present
         in chat_live.html.heex:4-5 for the chat surface; mirror them
         here so MeetingHooks' push_events have a listener. --%>
      <%!-- data-camera-enabled / data-mic-enabled: committed media intent the
         JS hook reads on a LiveKit reconnect to re-engage media without a
         fresh LV push (task 019e4b3e stage 3). MeetingHooks.attach/1 owns
         :camera_enabled / :mic_enabled here just as it does in ChatLive. --%>
      <div
        id="meeting-client"
        phx-hook="MeetingClient"
        class="hidden"
        data-camera-enabled={to_string(@camera_enabled)}
        data-mic-enabled={to_string(@mic_enabled)}
      >
      </div>
      <div id="meeting-room" phx-hook="MeetingRoom" class="hidden"></div>
      <%!-- Media sink (task 019e420f stage 1): MeetingClient.attachTrack
         routes incoming `<audio>` elements to `#meeting-media`. Without
         this anchor it falls back to `document.body` — works but
         pollutes the top-level DOM. `phx-update="ignore"` keeps the LV
         from blowing away the children on re-render. --%>
      <div id="meeting-media" class="hidden" phx-update="ignore"></div>

      <%!-- Desktop pane-collapse persistence (task 019e40f2). Two
         instances of the SidebarCollapse hook, parameterized via
         `data-*` attrs so each instance owns its own localStorage key.
         Storage keys are namespaced under `suite.meeting.*` so they
         never collide with the shell sidebar key
         (`suite.sidebar.collapsed`). The hidden host divs carry
         `data-collapsed` so the hook can compare against the LV's
         current assign and only push a hydrate event when stored
         value differs. --%>
      <%!-- Stage 4 (task 019f498d): shell-only. Guests never persist pane
         collapse — no shell chrome to collapse against. --%>
      <div
        :if={@shell?}
        id="meeting-participant-rail-collapse-hook"
        phx-hook="SidebarCollapse"
        data-collapsed={if @participant_rail_collapsed, do: "true", else: "false"}
        data-storage-key="suite.meeting.participant_rail.collapsed"
        data-hydrate-event="meeting_participant_rail_hydrate"
        data-change-event="meeting_participant_rail_collapsed_changed"
        class="hidden"
      >
      </div>
      <div
        :if={@shell?}
        id="meeting-chat-pane-collapse-hook"
        phx-hook="SidebarCollapse"
        data-collapsed={if @chat_panel_collapsed, do: "true", else: "false"}
        data-storage-key="suite.meeting.chat_pane.collapsed"
        data-hydrate-event="meeting_chat_pane_hydrate"
        data-change-event="meeting_chat_pane_collapsed_changed"
        class="hidden"
      >
      </div>

      <%!-- Closed-captions preference persistence host (task 019f3f17
         stage 2). Split out of the `MeetingCaptions` hook because that
         hook mounts on `#meeting-captions`, which is
         `:if={@captions_visible}`-gated. On a page reload the server
         assign defaults to `false`, that div isn't in the DOM, and the
         localStorage-restore branch that used to live in MeetingCaptions
         never ran — so a viewer preference of "on" never survived a
         reload (stage 1 review finding). This always-mounted host
         reads/writes localStorage on `meeting_captions_persist` /
         `meeting_captions_hydrate` and has no visible UI. --%>
      <div
        id="meeting-captions-persist-host"
        phx-hook="MeetingCaptionsPersist"
        data-persist-key="suite.meeting.captions_visible"
        class="hidden"
      >
      </div>

      <%!-- Breadcrumb escape hatch (task 019f62c8 stage 3). The shared
         `PlatformWeb.CoreComponents.breadcrumb/1` from stage 2 — the
         deterministic "take me UP to root" affordance that complements
         route-resume's "take me back to where I was". Renders
         `Meetings › <meeting name>`, the root segment navigating to the
         Meetings list (/meetings) and the meeting name as the inert
         current page.

         SHELL CHROME ONLY: gated on `@shell?` (== `chrome_mode == :shell`).
         The guest `meet.` surface (`chrome_mode == :none`, `:in_meeting`)
         reaches this SAME `@meeting != nil` shell tree, so without this
         gate a guest would get an authenticated back-to-list link into a
         surface they can't access. Mirrors the `@shell?` gating already
         applied to the chat panel / copy-guest-link / rail-collapse hooks. --%>
      <.breadcrumb
        :if={@shell?}
        segments={[
          %{label: "Meetings", navigate: ~p"/meetings"},
          %{label: @meeting.name, navigate: nil}
        ]}
      />

      <%!-- ── Top bar ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── --%>
      <%!-- TOP-BAR WIDTH BUDGET (task 019fb955-f8b1 rework). Everything in this
         header competes for one 390px-wide line on a phone, and before this
         rework the competition had no rules: the meeting title was the ONLY
         shrinkable item, so it absorbed the entire deficit and rendered at
         width ZERO while the bar still overflowed to 457px inside a 390px
         viewport, pushing two stage-mode buttons off-screen entirely.

         Two things caused that, and both are fixed here:

           1. The title sat next to a `flex-1` SPACER. A spacer with
              `flex-basis: 0` contributes nothing to shrinking, so under
              overflow it collapsed to 0 and handed the whole deficit to the
              only `min-w-0` item — the title. The spacer is gone; the title
              itself now grows (`grow basis-[50%]`), which right-aligns the
              rest exactly as the spacer did while giving the title a real
              proportional share to shrink FROM.

           2. The stage-mode control claimed in its own comment to "scroll on
              narrow viewports so it never overflows the top bar", but carried
              `flex-shrink-0`, which made that impossible. It was the single
              largest fixed block in the bar. It may now shrink and actually
              use its `overflow-x-auto`.

         Constraints are expressed in the LAYOUT'S OWN CURRENCY (percentages of
         this flex line), NOT in pixels: a px floor inside a proportional
         layout is how an earlier defect in this stack happened — the floor
         cannot see the container it is overflowing.

         The identity badges are INFORMATIONAL, the media buttons are
         CONTROLS, so the badges yield first: capped at 25% and truncating,
         while `[data-role="meeting-controls"]` keeps `flex-shrink-0` and its
         44px touch targets. Acceptance bar: no horizontal PAGE scroll at
         390px, every control reachable, and no element at width 0. --%>
      <header class="flex h-12 flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-2 border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200/60 px-3 sm:gap-3">
        <%!-- Meeting name. `grow basis-[50%] min-w-0` replaces the old
           auto-width box + `flex-1` spacer pair: same right-alignment for
           everything after it, but the title now has a proportional share of
           the line instead of being the residue of everyone else's. --%>
        <div
          class="flex min-w-0 shrink grow basis-[50%] items-center gap-2"
          data-role="meeting-title"
        >
          <span class="hero-video-camera size-5 flex-shrink-0 text-primary"></span>
          <h1 class="truncate text-sm font-semibold">{@meeting.name}</h1>
        </div>

        <%!-- Guest identity badge (task 019f498d stage 4). Only rendered on
           the guest surface (`chrome_mode == :none`) so the guest can see
           which name others in the meeting see. Mirrors the badge in
           guest_meeting_live.html.heex:117-119 verbatim so stage 5's
           router flip is a byte-identical visual delta.

           NARROWED to `and @state == :in_meeting` (task 019fb955-f8b1
           stage 5). `chrome_mode == :none` alone used to mean "guest",
           because the chrome-less layout only ever served the token guest
           flow. The `:meet_member` live_session is chrome-less too, and
           for an invited ACCOUNT this badge rendered a blank name and the
           wrong noun — literally " (guest)" after an empty interpolation.
           `:state` is what actually distinguishes the two: the guest mount
           clause sets it, the account mount leaves it nil. --%>
        <%!-- WIDTH: `max-w-[25%] min-w-0` + an inner `truncate`. This badge is
           byte-identical markup to the member badge below, and it had the
           SAME defect first: a `badge` sets `white-space: nowrap`, so as a
           flex item its min-content width IS its full text width and it could
           not shrink at all — it took its 126px off the top and the title
           paid. A guest on a phone has never been able to read the meeting
           name either; the member badge did not introduce this, it merely
           carried it to account holders. Fixed on BOTH surfaces. --%>
        <span
          :if={@chrome_mode == :none and @state == :in_meeting}
          class="badge badge-ghost min-w-0 max-w-[25%] shrink gap-1 overflow-hidden text-xs"
          data-role="guest-identity-badge"
        >
          <span class="hero-user size-3.5 flex-shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="truncate">{@display_name} (guest)</span>
        </span>

        <%!-- Member identity badge (task 019fb955-f8b1 stage 5). Sibling of
           the guest badge for the OTHER chrome-less surface: an invited
           external account on the `meet.` host, which reaches the general
           `mount/3` and so carries `@state == nil`. Same visual treatment,
           the account's own name, and NO "(guest)" suffix — this viewer is
           a Suite account, and labelling them a guest would misstate both
           who they are and how they got in. Rendered only when the name
           resolves; `member_display_name/1` returns nil when there is no
           account behind the mount, and a badge naming nobody is worse
           than no badge. --%>
        <%!-- WIDTH: identical treatment to the guest badge above — see the
           note there. Measured at 390x844 before this rework: this badge was
           125.9px and collapsed the meeting-title element to width 0 (390 →
           0/31, 430 → 0/71, 768 → 0/87.7 with the badge shown vs hidden;
           innocent at 1280, where 214.9/214.9 is unchanged). --%>
        <span
          :if={@chrome_mode == :none and is_nil(@state) and @display_name}
          class="badge badge-ghost min-w-0 max-w-[25%] shrink gap-1 overflow-hidden text-xs"
          data-role="member-identity-badge"
        >
          <span class="hero-user size-3.5 flex-shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="truncate">{@display_name}</span>
        </span>

        <%!-- Local-media controls + Join/Leave CTA. Visual treatment
           mirrors meeting_bar_live.ex's mini-bar: hero-microphone /
           hero-video-camera glyphs, base-content/70 when active, error-
           toned when muted/off, base-content/40 when off. Each button
           pushes the matching MeetingHooks event — the LiveKit lifecycle
           lives there (see meeting_hooks.ex), no client-side state
           here. --%>
        <div class="flex flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-1" data-role="meeting-controls">
          <%!-- Agents-muted badge (task 019fbaac stage 3). State is
             VISIBLE, not merely settable.

             It lives here in the top bar rather than in the intents
             collapse because that bar is gated on `@intents_visible?`
             and leaves the DOM entirely when the `meeting_producer`
             role is unassigned — so a mute indicator inside it would be
             invisible to exactly the person waiting on an agent that a
             since-departed participant silenced. This cluster renders
             for every viewer of the meeting shell (it is NOT `@shell?`-
             gated; only the breadcrumb and the guest-link controls
             are), so the guest surface inherits the badge too.

             Colour is never the only signal: the glyph and the word
             "muted" carry it without the amber.

             The word is `sr-only sm:not-sr-only` — announced to
             assistive tech at EVERY width, painted from `sm` up.
             Measured, not guessed: at 375px this top bar already
             overflows by 16px before the badge exists (scrollWidth 391
             vs clientWidth 375, which is why `meeting-copy-guest-link`
             below is `hidden sm:inline-flex`). The full-width badge is
             112px and took the bar to 507, pushing Screen, Gallery and
             Whiteboard off the viewport. Glyph-only it is 36px and the
             bar is 431.

             ATTRIBUTION, CORRECTED (task 019fbaac stage 4; the stage-3
             version of this comment got it wrong and it is fixed here
             rather than left to mislead the next reader). The
             pre-existing 16px is real, but its casualty is
             `chat-panel-toggle` alone — 12 of its 28px are inside the
             viewport before any of this. `Whiteboard` was NOT already
             clipping: unmuted it measures x 309 → right 351, fully
             visible. It clips only when muted (349 → 391). So the
             badge's ~40px is the proximate cause of Whiteboard
             clipping, and of `chat-panel-toggle` going from
             partly-clipped to entirely off screen. Honest statement:
             the bar's own debt costs `chat-panel-toggle`; this badge
             additionally costs `Whiteboard`.

             RESOLVED by the fix the stage-5 e2e review forced (case 1
             of validation 019fbab7-d432-795c-8f87-312e14985889). The
             overflow strategy this paragraph asked for exists now, so
             the badge no longer costs anyone their controls: the
             stage-mode segmented control below dropped
             `flex-shrink-0`, making it the bar's single flexible
             region: it absorbs the squeeze into its own scroll port
             instead of pushing the row's last item off screen. At 375px
             the bar is now scrollWidth 375 / clientWidth 375 in BOTH
             states (was 391 unmuted, 431 muted), `chat-panel-toggle`
             shows all 28 of its 28px with its centre hit-testable (was
             12, then −28), and `Whiteboard` is inside the scroll port
             rather than clipped by the viewport. The cost lands on the
             segmented control, which shrinks to a ~73px (unmuted) /
             ~52px (muted) scroll port at 375px and scrolls to the rest
             — a control that degrades by the mechanism its own markup
             advertises, not one that disappears.

             So: do not "fix" a future top-bar squeeze by making this
             badge conditional on width. Hiding the mute indicator on
             the viewport most likely to be a phone is precisely the
             reveal-over-persistence pattern Marlowe's 019f340a ruling
             bans, and it would put the state back out of sight for the
             viewer waiting on a silenced agent.

             A lone amber eye-slash is still not colour on its own. --%>
          <.chip
            :if={@intent_muted?}
            tone="warning"
            class="gap-1 font-mono text-[10px]"
            data-role="meeting-intent-mute-badge"
            title="Agents are muted for this meeting"
          >
            <span class="hero-eye-slash size-3.5" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="sr-only sm:not-sr-only">Agents muted</span>
          </.chip>

          <button
            type="button"
            phx-click="meeting_toggle_mic"
            aria-label={if @mic_enabled, do: "Mute mic", else: "Unmute mic"}
            aria-pressed={to_string(@mic_enabled)}
            title={if @mic_enabled, do: "Mute mic", else: "Unmute mic"}
            data-role="meeting-mic"
            data-active={to_string(@mic_enabled)}
            class={[
              "btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square min-h-11",
              if(@mic_enabled,
                do: "text-base-content/70",
                else: "text-error bg-error/10 hover:bg-error/20"
              )
            ]}
          >
            <span class={[
              "size-4",
              if(@mic_enabled, do: "hero-microphone", else: "hero-microphone-slash")
            ]}>
            </span>
          </button>

          <button
            type="button"
            phx-click="meeting_toggle_camera"
            aria-label={if @camera_enabled, do: "Turn off camera", else: "Turn on camera"}
            aria-pressed={to_string(@camera_enabled)}
            title={if @camera_enabled, do: "Turn off camera", else: "Turn on camera"}
            data-role="meeting-camera"
            data-active={to_string(@camera_enabled)}
            class={[
              "btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square min-h-11",
              if(@camera_enabled,
                do: "text-base-content/70",
                else: "text-base-content/40"
              )
            ]}
          >
            <span class={[
              "size-4",
              if(@camera_enabled, do: "hero-video-camera", else: "hero-video-camera-slash")
            ]}>
            </span>
          </button>

          <%!-- The meeting-wide "Copy guest link" button lived here until
             task 019fb955-1d7b stage 1 removed it. It minted ONE
             forwardable URL per meeting from an OPTIONAL display name, so
             whoever opened it was unidentifiable: transcript speech could
             not be attributed and action items had no principal. Guest
             admission is now per-invite only — `Meetings.invite_guest/2`
             mints a token naming a single `meeting_invites` row, which is
             what makes a join attributable and one guest revocable
             without invalidating the others. Do not reintroduce a
             meeting-wide link here. --%>

          <%!-- task 019e420f stage 2: client-side hook keeps the click
             gesture alive across the call into getDisplayMedia(). --%>
          <button
            id="meeting-share-btn-topbar"
            phx-hook="MeetingShareButton"
            type="button"
            aria-label={if @screen_share_enabled, do: "Stop sharing screen", else: "Share screen"}
            aria-pressed={to_string(@screen_share_enabled)}
            title={if @screen_share_enabled, do: "Stop sharing screen", else: "Share screen"}
            data-role="meeting-screen-share"
            data-active={to_string(@screen_share_enabled)}
            class={[
              "btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square min-h-11 hidden sm:inline-flex",
              if(@screen_share_enabled,
                do: "text-primary",
                else: "text-base-content/40"
              )
            ]}
          >
            <span class="hero-computer-desktop size-4"></span>
          </button>

          <%!-- Closed-captions toggle (task 019f3f17 stage 1). Only rendered
             when the viewer is joined (mirrors the Leave-button gate
             below) since captions come from the LiveKit transcription
             stream, which only flows during an active session. Event name
             `toggle_captions_meeting` retains the `_meeting` suffix from
             its original role sidestepping a since-removed shell
             `toggle_captions` handler (deleted by task 019f43da). The
             MeetingCaptions JS hook + `meeting_live_test.exs` still
             reference this name, so renaming would compound the blast
             radius without changing behaviour. --%>
          <%= if @in_meeting do %>
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="toggle_captions_meeting"
              aria-label={if @captions_visible, do: "Hide captions", else: "Show captions"}
              aria-pressed={to_string(@captions_visible)}
              title={if @captions_visible, do: "Hide captions", else: "Show captions"}
              data-role="meeting-captions-toggle"
              data-active={to_string(@captions_visible)}
              class={[
                "btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square min-h-11",
                if(@captions_visible,
                  do: "text-primary",
                  else: "text-base-content/40"
                )
              ]}
            >
              <svg
                xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="currentColor"
                class="size-4"
              >
                <path d="M2 5a2 2 0 012-2h16a2 2 0 012 2v10a2 2 0 01-2 2H6l-4 4V5zm5 3a1 1 0 00-1 1v1a1 1 0 001 1h2a1 1 0 001-1V9a1 1 0 00-1-1H7zm7 0a1 1 0 00-1 1v1a1 1 0 001 1h2a1 1 0 001-1V9a1 1 0 00-1-1h-2z" />
              </svg>
            </button>
          <% end %>

          <%= if @in_meeting do %>
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="meeting_leave"
              data-role="meeting-leave"
              class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost min-h-11 gap-1.5 text-error hover:bg-error/10"
              title="Leave meeting"
            >
              <span class="hero-phone-x-mark size-4"></span>
              <span class="hidden sm:inline">Leave</span>
            </button>
          <% else %>
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="meeting_join"
              data-role="meeting-join"
              class="btn btn-sm btn-primary min-h-11 gap-1.5"
              title="Join meeting"
            >
              <span class="hero-video-camera size-4"></span>
              <span class="hidden sm:inline">Join meeting</span>
            </button>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <%!-- Stage-mode segmented control (ADR 0042). Four hero-icon buttons;
           clicking one pushes `set_stage_mode` with the target mode and
           the LV swaps `@current_stage_mode`. The active button gets the
           daisyui segmented-active treatment (`bg-base-200 text-primary`);
           switching is pure presentation — the other modes' underlying
           state is untouched.

           The control scrolls on narrow viewports so it never overflows the
           top bar — that was ALWAYS the claim here, and `flex-shrink-0` made
           it false. A shrink-0 flex item cannot be squeezed, so
           `overflow-x-auto` never engaged and the four buttons simply ran off
           the right edge (measured at 390px: Gallery ending at 415.9,
           Whiteboard at 456.9 — both outside the viewport and UNREACHABLE,
           with no page scroll to recover them). `min-w-0 shrink` is what
           makes the documented behaviour actually happen: the control gives
           up width under pressure and its own scroller keeps every mode
           reachable.

           The same defect was found independently on `main` during the
           stage-5 e2e review of task 019fbaac, with its own measurements;
           that account is kept below verbatim because it names a second
           casualty (the chat-panel drawer toggle) this one does not. NOTE
           that the "only flexible region" claim it makes is no longer true
           after this rollup: the meeting title and the identity badge are
           shrinkable too (see the header comments above). The conclusion —
           do not re-add `flex-shrink-0` — is unchanged and holds for both.

           THIS WAS THE TOP BAR'S ONLY FLEXIBLE REGION, and that is
           load-bearing. Until the stage-5 e2e review of task 019fbaac
           the class list here was `join flex-shrink-0 overflow-x-auto`,
           alongside a comment
           claiming "the control wraps/scrolls on narrow viewports so it
           never overflows the top bar". That claim was false and the
           `overflow-x-auto` was inert: `flex-shrink-0` pinned the
           control at its natural 165px, so `clientWidth == scrollWidth`
           and there was nothing to scroll. The bar overflowed instead —
           at 375px it measured scrollWidth 391 / clientWidth 375
           unmuted, and 431 once the agents-muted badge appeared. Every
           one of those overflow pixels landed on the LAST item in the
           row, `chat-panel-toggle`, which went from 12 of its 28px
           visible to −28. That control is the only affordance that
           opens the drawer holding the mute toggle, so muting became a
           one-way door on a phone: a fresh 375px load of an
           already-muted meeting had no route back to unmuting.

           Dropping `flex-shrink-0` (and stating `min-w-0`, which
           `overflow-x-auto` already implies, so the intent survives a
           future class edit) makes the control absorb the squeeze into
           its own scroll port instead of exporting it to its siblings.
           The four modes stay reachable by scrolling; every other
           control in the bar — including the drawer toggle — stays
           where it is. Nothing changes at widths where the bar fits,
           because flex-shrink only engages on overflow.

           Do NOT re-add `flex-shrink-0`, and do not solve a future
           top-bar squeeze by hiding the drawer toggle or the mute
           badge: per Marlowe's `persistent-controls-over-reveal` ruling
           (task 019f340a) controls stay visible and get disabled at
           boundaries rather than hidden, and a control that disappears
           as a direct consequence of being used is the worst case that
           ruling exists to forbid.

           `scrollbar-width: none` matches the horizontal scroll strips
           in tasks_live/chat_live: a classic 15px scrollbar inside this
           48px (`h-12`) bar would eat the row it is scrolling. --%>
        <div
          class="join min-w-0 shrink overflow-x-auto"
          style="scrollbar-width: none; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;"
          role="group"
          aria-label="Stage mode"
          data-stage-mode-control="active"
        >
          <button
            :for={mode <- @stage_modes}
            type="button"
            phx-click="set_stage_mode"
            phx-value-mode={mode}
            aria-label={stage_mode_label(mode)}
            aria-pressed={to_string(@current_stage_mode == mode)}
            title={stage_mode_label(mode)}
            data-stage-mode-button={mode}
            data-active={to_string(@current_stage_mode == mode)}
            class={[
              "join-item btn btn-sm min-h-11 gap-1.5 border-base-300",
              if(@current_stage_mode == mode,
                do: "bg-base-200 text-primary",
                else: "btn-ghost text-base-content/60"
              )
            ]}
          >
            <span class={[stage_mode_icon(mode), "size-4"]}></span>
            <span class="hidden sm:inline">{stage_mode_label(mode)}</span>
          </button>
        </div>

        <%!-- Chat-panel toggle (sibling stage 3). Mobile-only — `lg:hidden`.
           On desktop the right column is always visible (`hidden
           lg:flex` on the aside) so the toggle would be a no-op there.
           Hero-icon only (no emoji) per the intent-panel-host
           greppability + the e2e_behavior validation refusing emoji
           codepoints. --%>
        <button
          :if={@shell?}
          type="button"
          phx-click="toggle_chat_panel"
          aria-label="Toggle chat panel"
          aria-expanded={to_string(@chat_panel_open)}
          aria-controls="meeting-chat-drawer"
          title="Toggle chat panel"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square text-base-content/60 lg:hidden"
          data-role="chat-panel-toggle"
        >
          <span class="hero-chat-bubble-left-right size-5"></span>
        </button>
      </header>

      <%!-- ── Body: left rail + center stage + chat panel ─────────────────── --%>
      <div class="relative flex min-h-0 flex-1">
        <%!-- Persistent local self-view PARKING SLOT (task 019e42f6 v2).
           v1 rendered this as a visible bottom-right picture-in-picture
           tile, which Rowan rejected: the self-video free-floated over the
           gallery and broke the grid layout. v2 keeps the SAME stable,
           never-unmounted node (`id="meeting-local-self-view"`,
           `phx-update="ignore"`, `data-role="local-self-view"`) — the
           page-level `MeetingClient` hook owns the single persistent local
           <video> born here — but it is now a ZERO-SIZE, hidden, off-layout
           PARKING slot, NOT a visible UI element. On every `set_stage_mode`
           change the hook reparents (appendChild, never detach) the live
           <video> into the active mode's dock:
             * gallery → `[data-local-video-dock][data-dock-mode="gallery"]`
               inside the local `[data-gallery-cell]`
             * canvas / screen / whiteboard →
               `[data-local-video-dock][data-dock-mode="strip"]` in the
               bottom strip's `[data-section="humans"]`
           The video is parked back here ONLY in the brief window between a
           dock unmounting and the next docking — never visible.

           Contract coexistence: `data-role="local-self-view"` (this parking
           slot) is DISTINCT from task 019e420f's `data-screen-share-slot`
           (shared-screen target) and from the new `data-local-video-dock`
           reparent targets. All three can be present simultaneously.
           Hidden entirely when the viewer has no participant row. --%>
        <% self_view =
          local_self_view_participant(
            assigns[:current_participant],
            assigns[:chrome_mode] == :none and assigns[:state] == :in_meeting,
            assigns[:display_name],
            assigns[:identity]
          ) %>
        <div
          :if={self_view}
          id="meeting-local-self-view"
          phx-update="ignore"
          class="hidden"
          data-role="local-self-view"
        >
        </div>

        <%!-- Left rail (task 019e2475-4395): sectioned participant list.
           Hosts/humans on top with mic + speaking + host-badge cues;
           agents below a divider with role chip + status dot.

           Task 019e40f2 — desktop collapse: two presentations on
           `>=lg`, mutually exclusive via `@participant_rail_collapsed`:
             * expanded (default): the existing w-60 aside, with a
               chevron-double-left button in the header that pushes
               `toggle_participant_rail`.
             * collapsed: a separate slim w-12 rail showing a
               chevron-double-right + user-group icon + participant
               count. Two-state render avoids participant-row reflow
               and speaking-ring paint artifacts vs a width transition. --%>
        <aside
          :if={!@participant_rail_collapsed}
          class="hidden w-60 flex-shrink-0 flex-col border-r border-base-300 bg-base-200/40 lg:flex"
          data-pane="left-rail"
        >
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-base-300 px-3 py-2">
            <span class="hero-user-group size-4 text-base-content/60"></span>
            <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-base-content/50">
              Participants
            </span>
            <span class="ml-auto text-xs text-base-content/40" data-role="participant-count">
              {length(assigns[:participants] || [])}
            </span>
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="toggle_participant_rail"
              aria-label="Collapse participants pane"
              aria-expanded="true"
              title="Collapse"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs btn-square text-base-content/40 hover:text-base-content hidden lg:inline-flex"
              data-role="participant-rail-toggle"
            >
              <span class="hero-chevron-double-left size-4"></span>
            </button>
          </div>
          <div class="min-h-0 flex-1 overflow-y-auto">
            <.participant_rail
              participants={assigns[:participants] || []}
              agents_by_id={assigns[:agents_by_id] || %{}}
              meeting={@meeting}
              speaking_participant_ids={assigns[:speaking_participant_ids] || MapSet.new()}
            />
          </div>
        </aside>

        <%!-- Collapsed slim rail (task 019e40f2). Only renders on `>=lg`
           AND when `@participant_rail_collapsed` is true. Carries the
           same `data-pane="left-rail"` selector so consumers can find
           "the left rail in either state", plus `data-pane-collapsed`
           so tests + e2e can disambiguate. --%>
        <aside
          :if={@participant_rail_collapsed}
          class="hidden w-12 flex-shrink-0 flex-col items-center border-r border-base-300 bg-base-200/40 py-2 lg:flex"
          data-pane="left-rail"
          data-pane-collapsed="true"
        >
          <button
            type="button"
            phx-click="toggle_participant_rail"
            aria-label="Expand participants pane"
            aria-expanded="false"
            title="Expand participants"
            class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm btn-square text-base-content/60 hover:text-base-content"
            data-role="participant-rail-toggle"
          >
            <span class="hero-chevron-double-right size-4"></span>
          </button>
          <span class="hero-user-group mt-2 size-4 text-base-content/40" aria-hidden="true">
          </span>
          <span
            class="mt-1 text-[10px] font-semibold text-base-content/40"
            data-role="participant-count"
          >
            {length(assigns[:participants] || [])}
          </span>
        </aside>

        <%!-- Center stage. Renders one of four stage-mode components keyed
           off @current_stage_mode. The `data-stage-mode` attribute drives
           renderer selection and is the e2e/test contract — keep it
           stable. --%>
        <main
          class="relative flex min-w-0 flex-1 items-center justify-center bg-base-100 p-4"
          data-pane="center-stage"
          data-stage-mode={@current_stage_mode}
        >
          <%!-- Whiteboard host (task 019fcfcf stage 3). Rendered for EVERY
             stage mode, unconditionally, and never removed. The Excalidraw
             React root the `MeetingWhiteboard` hook mounts here holds the
             only copy of the in-progress drawing: unlike the screen-share
             and video tiles there is no `window.__livekit_room` to
             re-attach from, so if this node lived inside the `cond` below,
             switching to Gallery would fire `destroyed()` and the strokes
             would be gone.

             `phx-update="ignore"` + a stable id keeps morphdom off the
             hook-owned subtree. Note that is necessary but NOT sufficient:
             LiveView still merges ATTRIBUTES onto an ignored node (#664),
             so visibility must not be server-rendered here. There is
             deliberately no `data-active` attribute — the hook toggles
             `hidden` itself from the `meeting_stage_mode_changed`
             push_event, which is client-side truth. It starts hidden
             because a meeting always mounts in `:gallery`. --%>
          <div
            id="meeting-whiteboard-host"
            phx-hook="MeetingWhiteboard"
            phx-update="ignore"
            data-whiteboard-host
            class="absolute inset-0 z-10 hidden h-full w-full overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-base-100"
          >
          </div>

          <%!-- "Send to canvas" control (task 019fcfcf stage 5).

             A SIBLING of `#meeting-whiteboard-host`, not a child, for two
             reasons that both bite: the host is `phx-update="ignore"` so
             nothing server-rendered may live inside it (#664), and the hook
             wipes `el.innerHTML` when the island resolves (`_clearHost`), so
             a button rendered inside would simply be deleted.

             Clicking it IS a LiveView re-render — that is the point of the
             stage-3/4 invariant, and `meeting_live_test.exs` asserts the
             React root survives it. --%>
          <div
            :if={@current_stage_mode == :whiteboard}
            class="absolute right-3 top-3 z-20"
            data-whiteboard-toolbar
          >
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="whiteboard_export_start"
              class="btn btn-sm min-h-10 gap-2 shadow-lg"
              data-action="whiteboard-export"
              aria-label="Send the whiteboard to the meeting canvas"
            >
              <span class="hero-arrow-up-tray size-4"></span>
              <span class="hidden sm:inline">Send to canvas</span>
            </button>
          </div>

          <%!-- "Someone else saved" badge (task 019fcfcf stage 4). Passive by
             design: v1 is SINGLE-EDITOR, so a remote save is announced and
             NOTHING is applied over what the local user is drawing. Reload is
             a plain link, so it is a real full page load that re-runs the
             hook's restore — deliberately the user's choice, never automatic.

             It lives OUTSIDE `#meeting-whiteboard-host`, as a sibling: the
             host is `phx-update="ignore"` and nothing server-rendered may go
             inside it. Only shown in whiteboard mode — a badge about a
             surface you are not looking at is noise. --%>
          <div
            :if={@current_stage_mode == :whiteboard and @whiteboard_remote_version}
            class="absolute inset-x-0 top-20 z-20 mx-3 flex items-start justify-between gap-3 rounded-lg bg-info/95 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-info-content shadow-lg"
            data-whiteboard-remote-notice
          >
            <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="hero-arrow-path size-4 shrink-0"></span>
              <span>Whiteboard updated by someone else</span>
            </span>
            <span class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1">
              <a
                href={~p"/meetings/#{@meeting.id}"}
                class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs min-h-8"
                data-action="whiteboard-reload"
              >
                Reload
              </a>
              <button
                type="button"
                phx-click="dismiss_whiteboard_remote_notice"
                class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs min-h-8"
                data-action="whiteboard-dismiss-remote"
                aria-label="Dismiss whiteboard update notice"
              >
                <span class="hero-x-mark size-4"></span>
              </button>
            </span>
          </div>

          <%= cond do %>
            <% @current_stage_mode == :canvas and assigns[:meeting_canvas] -> %>
              <%!-- Canvas mode (active canvas). Reuse the chat canvas
                 renderer so the meeting's surface stays visually
                 consistent with how a canvas appears in chat. --%>
              <div
                class="flex h-full w-full justify-center overflow-y-auto"
                data-placeholder="center-stage"
                data-canvas-id={@meeting_canvas.id}
              >
                <div class="w-full max-w-3xl">
                  <.canvas_document
                    canvas={@meeting_canvas}
                    dom_id_base="meeting-stage"
                  />
                </div>
              </div>
            <% @current_stage_mode == :canvas -> %>
              <%!-- Canvas mode (empty state). Meeting has no canvas yet —
                 surface a single CTA that mints one in the bound chat
                 space and assigns it as the active meeting canvas. --%>
              <div
                class="flex h-full w-full flex-col items-center justify-center rounded-xl border border-dashed border-base-300 text-base-content/50"
                data-placeholder="center-stage"
                data-empty-state="canvas"
              >
                <span class="hero-rectangle-stack size-10 text-base-content/40"></span>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-medium text-base-content/70">No canvas yet</p>
                <p class="mt-0.5 max-w-xs text-center text-xs">
                  Meetings work best when there's something to point at. Spin one up to start sketching.
                </p>
                <button
                  type="button"
                  phx-click="create_meeting_canvas"
                  class="btn btn-primary btn-sm mt-4 min-h-11 gap-1.5"
                  data-action="create-meeting-canvas"
                >
                  <span class="hero-plus size-4"></span>
                  <span>Create canvas</span>
                </button>
              </div>
            <% @current_stage_mode == :whiteboard -> %>
              <%!-- Whiteboard mode fallback. The real drawing surface is the
                 `#meeting-whiteboard-host` overlay above; this branch is what
                 shows underneath it before the hook has anything to paint
                 (server-rendered first paint, JS disabled, or the island
                 fetch still in flight). It is NOT "coming soon" any more —
                 the `data-coming-soon="whiteboard"` marker is gone on
                 purpose, and its absence is asserted in meeting_live_test. --%>
              <div
                class="flex h-full w-full flex-col items-center justify-center rounded-xl border border-dashed border-base-300 text-base-content/40"
                data-placeholder="center-stage"
                data-whiteboard-fallback
              >
                <span class="hero-pencil-square size-10"></span>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-medium text-base-content/70">Whiteboard</p>
                <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs">Loading the drawing surface…</p>
              </div>
            <% @current_stage_mode == :screen -> %>
              <%!-- Screen mode. The active LiveKit screen-share track (any
                 participant) is attached client-side by the
                 `MeetingScreenShare` JS hook into `[data-screen-share-slot]`.
                 When nobody is sharing, the empty-state `[data-screen-share-empty]`
                 is visible and exposes a "Start sharing" CTA which forwards
                 to the page-level `MeetingClient` hook via push_event
                 `toggle-screen-share`. Mode-switch survival: the LiveKit
                 Room lives on `window.__livekit_room`, so flipping to
                 Gallery and back re-mounts this hook against the same
                 room and synchronously reattaches the existing track. --%>
              <div
                id="meeting-screen-stage"
                phx-hook="MeetingScreenShare"
                class="relative flex h-full w-full items-center justify-center overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-base-200"
                data-placeholder="center-stage"
                data-screen-share-active="false"
              >
                <%!-- Hook target: the JS attaches the <video> element here
                   when a screen-share track is found. Hidden until a
                   track lands so the empty-state shows through.

                   `phx-update="ignore"` + a stable id: the LV doesn't render
                   the <video> itself (the `MeetingScreenShare` hook injects
                   it), so without these morphdom discards the JS-owned child
                   on ANY re-render (closing the "Meeting started" flash via
                   `lv:clear-flash`, a presence/roster update, toggling the
                   chat panel). The hook's `updated()` re-sync only re-attaches
                   when it has NO track reference — but its reference survives
                   the patch, so a wiped <video> would never come back and the
                   shared screen vanished. Mirrors the quick-meet slot
                   (`#meeting-screen-share-slot-desktop`, partials.ex) and the
                   local-video docks fixed in #421. --%>
                <div
                  data-screen-share-slot
                  id="meeting-screen-share-slot"
                  phx-update="ignore"
                  class="absolute inset-0 hidden h-full w-full"
                >
                </div>

                <%!-- Empty state: visible when no screen-share is live. The
                   hook toggles `hidden` on this node when it (de)attaches. --%>
                <div
                  data-screen-share-empty
                  class="flex h-full w-full flex-col items-center justify-center text-base-content/50"
                >
                  <span class="hero-computer-desktop size-10 text-base-content/40"></span>
                  <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-medium text-base-content/70">
                    No active screen-share
                  </p>
                  <p class="mt-0.5 max-w-xs text-center text-xs">
                    Anyone in the meeting can share their screen. Drop a window in to walk the team through it.
                  </p>
                  <%!-- task 019e420f stage 2: hook owns the click so
                     `getDisplayMedia()` runs inside the user gesture.
                     Falls back to pushing `meeting_toggle_screen_share`
                     when no LiveKit room is attached. --%>
                  <button
                    id="meeting-share-btn-screen-cta"
                    phx-hook="MeetingShareButton"
                    type="button"
                    class="btn btn-primary btn-sm mt-4 min-h-11 gap-1.5"
                    data-action="start-screen-share"
                    data-role="meeting-share-button"
                  >
                    <span class="hero-arrow-up-tray size-4"></span>
                    <span>Start sharing</span>
                  </button>
                </div>
              </div>
            <% @current_stage_mode == :gallery -> %>
              <%!-- Gallery mode. Lay out one `<.video_tile>` per human
                 participant in a responsive grid. Agents are excluded —
                 Gallery is a face-grid and agents surface in the bottom
                 strip's right-hand section via `<.agent_tile>` (sibling
                 task 019e2475-0037). The same `MeetingVideoTile` hook
                 powers the bottom-strip tiles, so identity strings are
                 kept in sync via `gallery_identity/1`. --%>
              <div
                class="flex h-full w-full flex-col"
                data-placeholder="center-stage"
                data-gallery-tile-count={
                  length(gallery_participants(assigns[:participants] || []))
                }
              >
                <%= if gallery_participants(assigns[:participants] || []) == [] do %>
                  <div class="flex h-full w-full flex-col items-center justify-center rounded-xl border border-dashed border-base-300 text-base-content/40">
                    <span class="hero-squares-2x2 size-10"></span>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-medium text-base-content/70">
                      No participants yet
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs">Tiles appear as people join the meeting.</p>
                  </div>
                <% else %>
                  <div
                    class={[
                      "grid h-full w-full auto-rows-fr gap-2 overflow-y-auto p-1 place-items-center",
                      gallery_grid_class(
                        length(gallery_participants(assigns[:participants] || [])),
                        assigns[:chrome_mode] || :shell
                      )
                    ]}
                    data-gallery-grid
                    data-gallery-grid-shape={
                      gallery_grid_class(
                        length(gallery_participants(assigns[:participants] || [])),
                        assigns[:chrome_mode] || :shell
                      )
                    }
                  >
                    <%!-- Each cell is a `[data-gallery-cell]` size container
                       (container-type: size). The tile inside uses a
                       container query to pick its binding axis based
                       on cell aspect ratio — see the
                       `[data-gallery-cell] > .meeting-video-tile` CSS
                       block in `apps/platform/assets/css/app.css`. The
                       wrapper fills its grid cell so the container
                       query has dimensions to work with. --%>
                    <div
                      :for={p <- gallery_participants(assigns[:participants] || [])}
                      class="relative grid h-full w-full place-items-center"
                      data-gallery-cell
                    >
                      <%!-- v2 (task 019e42f6): for the LOCAL participant the
                         tile carries `data-local-dock` so the MeetingVideoTile
                         hook skips its own camera-attach (the single
                         persistent local <video> is owned + reparented by the
                         page-level MeetingClient hook). The gallery dock below
                         is the reparent TARGET in gallery mode — the hook
                         appendChilds the live <video> into it so the feed
                         re-occupies the slot allocated to this user.
                         Remote tiles attach their own track unchanged. --%>
                      <% local? = gallery_identity(p) == viewer_gallery_identity(assigns) %>
                      <%!-- `id_prefix="gallery-tile"` (not the shared default
                         `video-tile`) so the gallery tile and the bottom-strip
                         tile for the SAME participant have DISTINCT DOM ids.
                         The shared id used to encourage morphdom to move/reuse
                         the node across stage_mode swaps, but in practice the
                         move path dropped the injected remote `<video>` on
                         every gallery↔strip transition while the local
                         self-view (reparented separately by MeetingClient)
                         survived. Distinct ids → clean destroy-and-mount:
                         `_detach` runs cleanly on the old tile,
                         `_syncExistingTrack` rebinds the live track on the
                         freshly mounted tile in the new location. --%>
                      <%!-- Per-speaker caption overlay (task 019f3f17 stage 2).
                         `caption_slot?={true}` renders a
                         `[data-caption-slot]` div inside the tile that
                         MeetingCaptions writes into when a matching
                         `meeting:caption` event lands. Only gallery mode
                         opts in — the bottom strip's 96/120px tiles are
                         too short to fit a legible caption line, and the
                         central bar already carries the transcript in
                         canvas/screen/whiteboard modes. --%>
                      <.video_tile
                        id_prefix="gallery-tile"
                        participant={
                          %{
                            identity: gallery_identity(p),
                            name: p.display_name || "Unknown participant"
                          }
                        }
                        video_track={false}
                        audio_muted?={false}
                        speaking?={false}
                        avatar_url={Map.get(p, :avatar_url)}
                        layout={:gallery}
                        caption_slot?={@captions_visible}
                        data-local-dock={local? && "true"}
                      />
                      <%!-- Gallery local-video dock: the persistent local
                         <video> is reparented here on gallery mode. Absolutely
                         positioned to cover the local participant's gallery
                         cell so the reparented `object-cover` video re-occupies
                         the slot allocated to this user (no free-floating PiP).
                         It overlays the avatar-fallback tile beneath it — the
                         video paints over the avatar when the camera is live,
                         and the avatar shows through when the camera is off
                         (the dock is empty). Only the local cell renders one.
                         See the `data-local-video-dock` contract note near
                         `#meeting-local-self-view`. --%>
                      <%!-- Stable id + phx-update="ignore": the persistent
                         local <video> is appendChild'd here by MeetingClient.
                         This dock lives inside the server `:for` over gallery
                         participants, so without `ignore` ANY LV re-render
                         (closing the "Meeting started" flash, a presence
                         update, a panel toggle) lets morphdom replace the
                         node and discard the JS-injected <video>. There's
                         exactly one local cell, so a single stable id is
                         safe. Belt-and-suspenders with the hook's `updated()`
                         re-dock (which only fires when the hook's own element
                         is in the diff). --%>
                      <%!-- Sizing: dropped `absolute inset-0` so the dock no
                         longer overlays the WHOLE cell (which made the local
                         video render extra-wide vs the 16:9-constrained remote
                         tiles). CSS in app.css matches the dock to the same
                         16:9 + container-query sizing as `.meeting-video-tile`
                         under the same `[data-gallery-cell]` parent, so the
                         dock + remote tiles are pixel-aligned. --%>
                      <div
                        :if={local?}
                        id="meeting-local-video-dock-gallery"
                        phx-update="ignore"
                        class="pointer-events-none overflow-hidden rounded-lg"
                        data-local-video-dock
                        data-dock-mode="gallery"
                      >
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                <% end %>
              </div>
          <% end %>
        </main>

        <%!-- Right pane: embedded chat panel (sibling 019e2475-87a8).
           Shipped fully via #364: the embed itself, the framing
           (HEADER + ~320px desktop width), and the mobile drawer +
           top-bar toggle.

           Layout strategy — ONE aside, two presentations:
             * `>=lg` (desktop): in-flow right column. The aside is
               `lg:static lg:flex` etc. and IGNORES @chat_panel_open
               entirely, so the desktop pane never collapses.
             * `<lg` (mobile/tablet): the same aside is `fixed inset-y-0
               right-0` with `translate-x-full` by default and
               `translate-x-0` when @chat_panel_open. A backdrop element
               sits underneath when open; its phx-click also dispatches
               toggle_chat_panel for backdrop-dismiss.

           Critically the embedded `live_render` lives in EXACTLY ONE
           DOM node (this aside). Rendering it twice with the same id
           would collide; positioning the single node responsively keeps
           the embed canonical.

           The pane wrapper carries data-pane="chat-panel" and the
           *embedded LV* root carries data-intent-panel-host="true" for
           the future intent-panel migration (greppable hook). The
           header caption text "placeholder for intent panel (ADR 0042)"
           is also intentionally greppable for the same migration. --%>

        <%!-- Mobile drawer backdrop. Only on `<lg` AND only when the
           drawer is open. phx-click closes the drawer. Sits below the
           drawer (z-30) but above the meeting body. Shell-only (stage 4
           of task 019f498d) — the guest surface has no chat panel and
           therefore no drawer to backdrop. --%>
        <div
          :if={@shell? and @chat_panel_open}
          phx-click="toggle_chat_panel"
          aria-hidden="true"
          class="fixed inset-0 z-30 bg-black/30 backdrop-blur-sm lg:hidden"
          data-role="chat-panel-backdrop"
        >
        </div>

        <aside
          :if={@shell?}
          id="meeting-chat-drawer"
          class={
            [
              # Shared
              "flex flex-col border-l border-base-300 bg-base-100",
              # Mobile drawer (default <lg). Fixed slide-in from right with
              # safe-area-aware bottom edge (mirrors PR #335 / tasks_live
              # pattern). z-40 above the backdrop (z-30). max-w caps so a
              # phone landscape doesn't get a 100% drawer.
              "fixed top-12 right-0 z-40 w-80 max-w-[85vw] shadow-xl transition-transform duration-200 ease-out",
              "bottom-[calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom)+var(--bottom-nav-height,0px))]",
              if(@chat_panel_open, do: "translate-x-0", else: "translate-x-full"),
              # Desktop (>=lg): in-flow right column. The mobile drawer
              # path stays byte-identical; only the `lg:*` classes consult
              # `@chat_panel_collapsed`. When collapsed: `lg:!hidden`
              # overrides the shared `flex` so the wide pane disappears at
              # the desktop breakpoint while the embedded `live_render`
              # stays in the DOM (preserves chat scroll + message stream).
              "lg:static lg:top-auto lg:bottom-auto lg:right-auto lg:z-auto lg:max-w-none lg:flex-shrink-0 lg:translate-x-0 lg:shadow-none lg:transition-none",
              if(@chat_panel_collapsed, do: "lg:!hidden", else: "lg:w-80")
            ]
          }
          data-pane="chat-panel"
          data-chat-panel-open={to_string(@chat_panel_open)}
        >
          <%!-- Pane header: meeting name + chat icon. The verbose
             "placeholder for intent panel" caption is gone now that the
             pane hosts the real chat surface (stage 5 of 019e2d1a). The
             greppable migration marker `data-intent-panel-host="true"`
             is preserved on the body wrapper below so a future intent-
             panel swap-out can still find this surface with `rg`. --%>
          <header
            class="flex flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-2 border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200/40 px-3 py-2"
            data-pane-section="header"
          >
            <span class="hero-chat-bubble-left-right size-4 flex-shrink-0 text-base-content/60">
            </span>
            <h2 class="min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-sm font-semibold text-base-content">
              {@meeting.name}
            </h2>
            <%!-- Desktop-only collapse chevron (task 019e40f2). Hidden on
               `<lg` so the mobile drawer header is byte-identical;
               flips the wide chat pane to a slim 48px collapsed rail
               at the right edge. --%>
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="toggle_meeting_chat_pane"
              aria-label="Collapse chat pane"
              aria-expanded="true"
              title="Collapse"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs btn-square text-base-content/60 hidden lg:inline-flex"
              data-role="chat-pane-toggle"
            >
              <span class="hero-chevron-double-right size-4"></span>
            </button>
            <%!-- Mobile-only close button inside the drawer header. The
               top-bar toggle handles open; this gives a one-tap close
               that doesn't require backdrop-tap (e.g. for a wide
               drawer that covers the whole viewport). --%>
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="toggle_chat_panel"
              aria-label="Close chat panel"
              title="Close"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs btn-square text-base-content/60 lg:hidden"
              data-role="chat-panel-close"
            >
              <span class="hero-x-mark size-4"></span>
            </button>
          </header>

          <%!-- Intents-collapse bar (task 019f70b8 stage 7). A SIBLING
             above the chat body, not a replacement for it: the producer
             watches the intent queue and the discussion in the same
             rail. Absent from the DOM — not hidden — when the
             `meeting_producer` role is unassigned (AC13); an unfilled
             capability is not a denial, and there is nothing to reserve
             layout space for. --%>
          <.intents_collapse
            :if={@intents_visible?}
            intents={@intents}
            open={@intents_open}
            expanded={@intents_expanded}
            pending_cancels={@intent_pending_cancels}
            reason_picker_id={@intent_reason_picker_id}
            error={@intent_error}
            muted={@intent_muted?}
          />

          <%!-- Body wrapper: flex-1/min-h-0 lets the embedded LV consume
             the remaining vertical space *below* the header, while its
             own internal scroll region (overflow-y-auto on the messages
             list) stays clamped to that space. Without min-h-0 the
             messages list would be unable to shrink and would push the
             composer off-screen.

             Stage 5 (019e2d1a) swap: the stub `MeetingChatPanelLive`
             is gone — we now `live_render` the real `PlatformWeb.ChatLive`
             in its embedded mode, which seeds the active space from the
             session and reuses the full chat affordance set (mentions,
             attachments, reactions, threading, edits, link previews,
             canvases). The intent-panel migration marker
             (`data-intent-panel-host="true"`) is preserved on this
             wrapper so future-Rowan can still grep for the swap-out
             site when the real intent panel ships. --%>
          <div
            class="flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col"
            data-pane-section="body"
            data-intent-panel-host="true"
            data-pane="meeting-chat-panel"
          >
            <%= if @meeting.chat_space && @meeting.chat_space.slug do %>
              {live_render(@socket, PlatformWeb.ChatLive.Embedded,
                id: "meeting-chat-panel-#{@meeting.id}",
                session: %{
                  "chat_space_slug" => @meeting.chat_space.slug,
                  "current_user_id" => @user_id
                }
              )}
            <% else %>
              <div
                class="flex h-full items-center justify-center text-xs text-base-content/40"
                data-placeholder="chat-panel-empty"
              >
                <span class="hero-chat-bubble-left-right size-5"></span>
                <span class="ml-2">No discussion bound to this meeting.</span>
              </div>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <%!-- Collapsed slim chat rail (task 019e40f2). Mirror image of
           the participant collapsed rail: only on `>=lg` AND when
           `@chat_panel_collapsed`. Carries `data-pane="chat-panel"`
           + `data-pane-collapsed="true"` so consumers can find "the
           chat pane in either state". --%>
        <aside
          :if={@shell? and @chat_panel_collapsed}
          class="hidden w-12 flex-shrink-0 flex-col items-center border-l border-base-300 bg-base-200/40 py-2 lg:flex"
          data-pane="chat-panel"
          data-pane-collapsed="true"
        >
          <button
            type="button"
            phx-click="toggle_meeting_chat_pane"
            aria-label="Expand chat pane"
            aria-expanded="false"
            title="Expand chat"
            class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm btn-square text-base-content/60 hover:text-base-content"
            data-role="chat-pane-toggle"
          >
            <span class="hero-chevron-double-left size-4"></span>
          </button>
          <span
            class="hero-chat-bubble-left-right mt-2 size-4 text-base-content/40"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
          </span>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <%!-- Bottom strip (task 019e2475-0037): horizontal flex row composing
         one `<.video_tile>` per human (left) and one `<.agent_tile>` per
         agent (right), with a thin vertical divider between sections.
         Strip height: 96px on `<lg`, 120px on `lg`+. Bottom edge respects
         `safe-area-inset-bottom` so the iOS home indicator never overlaps
         the tiles.

         Gallery-default reframe (stage 3, task 019e2d1a): the strip is
         hidden in gallery mode so the responsive grid owns the full
         vertical space. It re-renders in canvas / screen / whiteboard
         modes as the compact "who's here" footer. Identity strings are
         deliberately aligned with gallery (`"user:" <> pid` instead of
         `"strip:user:" <> pid`) so the MeetingVideoTile hook reuses the
         same DOM node across mode swaps and the LiveKit track stays
         attached during the layout flip. --%>
      <footer
        :if={@current_stage_mode != :gallery}
        id="meeting-bottom-strip"
        class={[
          "flex flex-shrink-0 items-stretch gap-2 overflow-x-auto overscroll-contain",
          "border-t border-base-300 bg-base-200/40 px-2 py-2",
          "h-[96px] lg:h-[120px]",
          "pb-[calc(0.5rem+env(safe-area-inset-bottom))]"
        ]}
        data-pane="bottom-strip"
        data-tile-count-humans={length(strip_humans(assigns[:participants]))}
        data-tile-count-agents={length(strip_agents(assigns[:participants]))}
      >
        <%!-- Human section: one `<.video_tile>` per human participant.
           Empty-state stays silent so the strip doesn't churn when humans
           join/leave. --%>
        <section
          class="flex h-full items-stretch gap-2"
          data-section="humans"
          aria-label="Participants"
        >
          <%!-- v2 (task 019e42f6): each human gets a strip tile. The LOCAL
             human's tile is wrapped so the strip local-video dock can overlay
             it; the tile carries `data-local-dock` so MeetingVideoTile skips
             its own camera-attach (the persistent local <video> is reparented
             here by MeetingClient in canvas/screen/whiteboard modes). Remote
             humans render an unwrapped tile that attaches its own track. --%>
          <div
            :for={participant <- strip_humans(assigns[:participants])}
            class="relative h-full shrink-0"
          >
            <% local? = gallery_identity(participant) == viewer_gallery_identity(assigns) %>
            <%!-- `id_prefix="strip-tile"` mirrors `gallery-tile` above; see the
               rationale on the gallery `<.video_tile>` for why we deliberately
               give the two surfaces DIFFERENT dom ids per participant. --%>
            <PlatformWeb.Components.Meeting.VideoTile.video_tile
              id_prefix="strip-tile"
              participant={
                %{
                  identity: gallery_identity(participant),
                  name: participant.display_name || "Unknown participant"
                }
              }
              video_track={false}
              audio_muted?={!Map.get(participant.attention_config || %{}, "mic_on", false)}
              speaking?={false}
              avatar_url={participant.avatar_url}
              data-local-dock={local? && "true"}
            />
            <%!-- Strip local-video dock: the reparent target in
               canvas/screen/whiteboard modes. `absolute inset-0` over the
               aspect-video strip tile so the reparented video fills it. --%>
            <%!-- Stable id + phx-update="ignore" so morphdom never discards
               the JS-injected local <video> on an LV re-render. Exactly one
               local strip cell, so a single stable id is safe. See the
               gallery dock rationale above. --%>
            <div
              :if={local?}
              id="meeting-local-video-dock-strip"
              phx-update="ignore"
              class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 overflow-hidden rounded-lg"
              data-local-video-dock
              data-dock-mode="strip"
            >
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <%!-- Vertical divider: only when BOTH sections have at least one
           tile. --%>
        <div
          :if={
            strip_humans(assigns[:participants]) != [] and
              strip_agents(assigns[:participants]) != []
          }
          class="h-full w-px flex-shrink-0 self-stretch border-l border-base-300"
          data-role="strip-divider"
          aria-hidden="true"
        >
        </div>

        <%!-- Agent section: one `<.agent_tile>` per agent participant. --%>
        <section
          class="flex h-full items-stretch gap-2"
          data-section="agents"
          aria-label="Agent presence"
        >
          <PlatformWeb.Components.Meeting.AgentTile.agent_tile
            :for={participant <- strip_agents(assigns[:participants])}
            agent_participant={
              %{
                identity: "agent:" <> (participant.participant_id || ""),
                id: participant.id,
                name: participant.display_name || "Agent"
              }
            }
            role={agent_role(participant)}
            activity_state={:idle}
            accent_color={nil}
            avatar_url={participant.avatar_url}
          />
        </section>
      </footer>
    </div>

    <%!-- ── Guest-invite bottom-sheet (stage 4 of task 019f2483) ────────────
       Host-only surface: the trigger button in the top-bar is gated on
       `host?/2`, but the sheet itself is unconditionally scoped inside
       the `:show` branch — a non-host with no trigger will never see
       `:show_guest_invite_sheet` flip to true.

       Two visual states:
         1. Pre-generation: two form fields (Guest name, Expiry) + a
            "Generate link" submit.
         2. Post-generation (`@guest_invite_url` non-nil): a read-only
            URL with an inline Copy button (CopyToClipboard hook) and a
            helper line explaining the display_name mode.

       Mirrors the start-meeting sheet's mobile-first geometry: slides
       up from the bottom on narrow viewports (`items-end`), centered
       card on `>=sm`. Backdrop click closes the sheet. --%>
    <%!-- Intents-collapse overlays (task 019f70b8 stage 7). Deliberately
       rendered HERE, at the LV root, and not inside the strip: the chat
       rail carries `transition-transform` so it can slide in as a mobile
       drawer, and a transformed ancestor becomes the containing block
       for `position: fixed`. An overlay rendered inside the strip is
       centred on a 320px rail instead of on the viewport. --%>
    <.intents_overlays
      :if={@intents_visible?}
      intents={@intents}
      expanded={@intents_expanded}
      edit_row={@intent_edit_row}
      agent_options={@intent_agent_options}
    />

    <%!-- ── Closed-captions overlay (task 019f3f17 stage 1 + stage 2) ────
       The MeetingCaptions JS hook binds here, listens for `meeting:caption`
       CustomEvents dispatched by MeetingRoom, and renders up to
       MAX_LINES=3 speaker+text lines with the interim-vs-final treatment.
       `phx-update="ignore"` prevents LV morphdom from clearing the
       hook-written innerHTML on unrelated re-renders. Positioned as a
       fixed overlay centered above the mobile bottom-nav; the
       `--bottom-nav-height` shell CSS var expands to 56px on phones and
       0 on desktop so this bar clears the nav automatically. Rendered
       ONLY when the viewer has toggled captions on, so it stays out of
       the DOM (and out of JS event listeners) at rest.

       Stage 2: `z-40` (was `z-30`) so the caption bar reliably paints
       above the mobile bottom-nav rail (`z-30`) — the stage-1 reviewer
       flagged a mobile z-index collision where the 3-line caption bar
       overlapped the sticky nav on narrow viewports. Also, the persist-
       key + persistence branch moved to the always-mounted
       `#meeting-captions-persist-host` (see above) so a page reload with
       the server default `:captions_visible = false` still restores the
       viewer's stored preference. --%>
    <%!-- Gallery-mode caption fan-out: when the stage is in gallery mode we
       hide the combined caption bar via `hidden` (display:none) rather
       than dropping it from the DOM with `:if`. The `MeetingCaptions`
       hook does DOUBLE duty — it renders the central rolling buffer AND
       fans each `meeting:caption` segment out to per-tile
       `[data-participant] [data-caption-slot]` overlays. Removing the
       host element unmounts the hook and would silence the per-tile
       overlays too, regressing the gallery-view UX this task exists to
       preserve.

       Two-div structure — the OUTER `#meeting-captions-shell` carries the
       positioning classes AND the conditional `hidden` toggle. The INNER
       `#meeting-captions` div is the stable hook host (`phx-update="ignore"`
       + `phx-hook="MeetingCaptions"`). Splitting them matters: with
       `phx-update="ignore"` on the hook host, Phoenix's morphdom (and the
       LV test client, see `Phoenix.LiveViewTest.TreeDOM.apply_phx_update/4`)
       preserves NON-`data-*` attributes across renders, so the `hidden`
       class flip has to live on an element the LV freely re-renders. The
       wrapper's `data-stage-mode` attribute mirrors the assign so CDP e2e
       and tests can query it. --%>
    <div
      :if={@captions_visible}
      data-role="meeting-captions-shell"
      data-stage-mode={@current_stage_mode}
      class={[
        "fixed bottom-[calc(1rem+env(safe-area-inset-bottom)+var(--bottom-nav-height,0px))] left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 z-40 w-full max-w-2xl px-4 pointer-events-none",
        @current_stage_mode == :gallery && "hidden"
      ]}
    >
      <div
        id="meeting-captions"
        phx-hook="MeetingCaptions"
        phx-update="ignore"
        data-role="meeting-captions"
        class="w-full py-3 rounded-xl bg-black/75 backdrop-blur-sm text-sm text-white shadow-lg transition-opacity duration-300"
        aria-live="polite"
        aria-label="Meeting captions"
      >
      </div>
    </div>
  <% true -> %>
    <%!-- `:index` action — grouped meetings list (live / upcoming / ended).
       Empty state when zero meetings exist; otherwise a vertical stack
       of sections, each with an icon + label header and one row per
       meeting linking to `/meetings/<slug>`. The "Start meeting" FAB
       is owned by stage 2 and lands later — the empty-state CTA below
       is the only action surface in this stage. --%>
    <%!-- Resolved ONCE for the whole arm (task 019fb954-8ead stage 4).
       The desktop panel and the mobile sheet render the same meeting and
       the same invitee rows; deriving each of them where it is used would
       issue the invite queries twice per render and let the two surfaces
       disagree if one call ever picked up different options. --%>
    <% detail_meeting = selected_meeting(assigns) %>
    <% detail_invites = (detail_meeting && detail_invite_rows(detail_meeting)) || [] %>
    <div
      class="flex h-full min-h-0 flex-col bg-base-100 text-base-content"
      data-page="meetings-index"
    >
      <header class="flex h-12 flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-2 border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200/60 px-3 sm:gap-3">
        <div class="flex min-w-0 items-center gap-2">
          <span class="hero-video-camera size-5 flex-shrink-0 text-primary"></span>
          <h1 class="truncate text-sm font-semibold">Meetings</h1>
        </div>

        <%!-- ── Calendar · Archive toggle (task 019fb954-c848) ────────────
           URL state, not an assign: `?view=archive` survives a reload, a
           pasted link and the back button. The calendar half is the
           default, so its segment drops every archive parameter rather
           than carrying a dead `view=calendar` around. Labels shorten to
           `Cal` on phones per the mobile frame; both segments are 44px
           tall at every width. --%>
        <div
          class="flex flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-0.5 rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-100 p-0.5"
          data-role="meetings-view-toggle"
          role="group"
          aria-label="Meetings view"
        >
          <.link
            patch={~p"/meetings"}
            data-view="calendar"
            aria-current={@meetings_view == :calendar && "page"}
            class={[
              "flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-md px-3 text-xs font-medium transition sm:min-h-9",
              @meetings_view == :calendar &&
                "bg-base-200 text-base-content shadow-sm",
              @meetings_view != :calendar && "text-base-content/60 hover:text-base-content"
            ]}
          >
            <span class="sm:hidden">Cal</span>
            <span class="hidden sm:inline">Calendar</span>
          </.link>
          <.link
            patch={archive_path(assigns)}
            data-view="archive"
            aria-current={@meetings_view == :archive && "page"}
            class={[
              "flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-md px-3 text-xs font-medium transition sm:min-h-9",
              @meetings_view == :archive &&
                "bg-base-200 text-base-content shadow-sm",
              @meetings_view != :archive && "text-base-content/60 hover:text-base-content"
            ]}
          >
            Archive
          </.link>
        </div>

        <div class="flex-1"></div>

        <%!-- Counts what the GRID DRAWS, not what the LiveView loaded.
           This read `length(@meetings) total` — a binding left over from
           the meetings LIST this surface used to be, where `@meetings`
           was the thing on screen and the count was honest. The calendar
           replaced the list and the chip kept pointing at the old
           collection, so it counted rows the grid deliberately refuses to
           place (cancelled, and never-scheduled) and reported 7 beside 5
           blocks. Worse than stale: `@meetings` is the whole viewer-scoped
           index list, unbounded in time — the archive half already refuses
           to source from it for that reason. (It is viewer-scoped as of
           019f9cb7; it was not when this chip was written.) --%>
        <%!-- The header count makes the SAME false assertion the day count
           does — "No meetings this week" while the week was never
           queried — and it is the one a viewer reads first. Suppressing
           the day count and leaving this one would fix the smaller
           instance of the bug and leave the larger. Not in the stage
           brief; found in a measured screenshot of the brief's own
           output. --%>
        <span
          :if={@meetings_view == :calendar and @calendar_zone_unresolved?}
          class="text-xs text-base-content/50"
          data-role="meetings-count"
          data-count-state="unknown"
        >
          Week not placed
        </span>

        <span
          :if={@meetings_view == :calendar and !@calendar_zone_unresolved?}
          class="text-xs text-base-content/50"
          data-role="meetings-count"
        >
          {calendar_week_count_label(
            calendar_week_meeting_count(
              @calendar_week_meetings,
              @calendar_anchor_date,
              @calendar_time_zone,
              @calendar_hour_window
            )
          )}
        </span>

        <%!-- MATCHED of total, not a bare total — see `archive_count_label/2`.
           Desktop only: the mobile frame moves this string onto the results
           section heading, so on a phone it renders there instead. --%>
        <span
          :if={@meetings_view == :archive}
          class="hidden text-xs text-base-content/50 sm:inline"
          data-role="archive-count"
        >
          {archive_count_label(@archive_result, "filtered")}
        </span>

        <%!-- ── Desktop `New meeting` button (task 019fb954-8ead stage 5) ──
           The create path the frames put in the toolbar at `lg`, where a
           corner FAB is the wrong affordance for a pointer surface. It
           REUSES `open_start_meeting_sheet` — the same event the FAB
           pushes, the same sheet — so there is one create flow with two
           entry points rather than two flows that can drift.

           `hidden lg:inline-flex` is the exact inverse of the FAB's
           `lg:hidden`: below `lg` this is not drawn and the FAB is the
           only create path; at `lg` and above they swap. --%>
        <button
          type="button"
          phx-click="open_start_meeting_sheet"
          data-role="new-meeting-button"
          class="btn btn-primary btn-sm hidden lg:inline-flex gap-1.5"
        >
          <span class="hero-plus size-4"></span> New meeting
        </button>
      </header>

      <%!-- ── Archive pane (task 019fb954-c848) ───────────────────────────
         The index's second half. The `:if={@meetings_view == :calendar}`
         guards on the empty-state and `data-region="meetings-list"` blocks
         below are the ONLY edit this task makes to the calendar side — the
         two panes are mutually exclusive, expressed as a guard on each
         root rather than as a wrapper so the calendar half's markup keeps
         its exact shape and indentation for the sibling that owns it.

         "Also in this range, no match" is the canvas's own heading, and it
         presupposes a date range that frame 4's filter bar does not
         contain (search, four artifact chips, person, project — no date
         picker). That is a real gap in the design, resolved deliberately
         rather than silently: "this range" here IS the full in-scope
         archive — every ended or cancelled meeting the viewer can see. A
         bounded range would be an additive control in the same bar and
         would not change this heading. --%>
      <div
        :if={@meetings_view == :archive}
        class="flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col"
        data-region="meetings-archive"
      >
        <%!-- Filter bar. Search and project are one form so a change to
           either carries the other into the URL; the chips are links for
           the same reason. Everything lands in `handle_params/3`, so a
           result set is a URL somebody else can open. --%>
        <form
          class="flex flex-shrink-0 flex-col gap-2 border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200/40 px-3 py-2 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:px-4"
          phx-change="archive_filter"
          phx-submit="archive_filter"
        >
          <label class="flex min-h-11 flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-100 px-3 sm:w-72">
            <span class="hero-magnifying-glass size-4 flex-shrink-0 text-base-content/40"></span>
            <input
              type="search"
              name="q"
              value={@archive_query}
              placeholder="Search meetings"
              autocomplete="off"
              aria-label="Search the meetings archive"
              phx-debounce="300"
              data-role="archive-search"
              class="w-full min-w-0 bg-transparent text-sm outline-none placeholder:text-base-content/40"
            />
          </label>

          <%!-- One row on every viewport; the phone SCROLLS it sideways
             rather than wrapping, so the results start at the same place
             regardless of how many chips there are. --%>
          <div
            class="-mx-3 flex items-center gap-1.5 overflow-x-auto px-3 sm:mx-0 sm:flex-wrap sm:overflow-visible sm:px-0"
            data-role="archive-filter-chips"
          >
            <.link
              :for={type <- Archive.types()}
              patch={archive_toggle_types_path(assigns, type)}
              data-role="archive-type-chip"
              data-type={type}
              data-active={to_string(type in @archive_types)}
              class={[
                "inline-flex min-h-11 flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-3 text-xs transition sm:min-h-9",
                type in @archive_types && "border-primary bg-primary text-primary-content",
                type not in @archive_types &&
                  "border-base-300 bg-base-100 text-base-content/60 hover:text-base-content"
              ]}
            >
              <span class={[archive_type_icon(type), "size-3.5 flex-shrink-0"]}></span>
              <span class="sm:hidden">{archive_type_short_label(type)}</span>
              <span class="hidden sm:inline">{archive_type_label(type)}</span>
              <span
                :if={type in @archive_types}
                class="font-mono text-[10px] opacity-75"
                data-role="archive-type-count"
              >
                {Map.get(@archive_result.type_counts, type, 0)}
              </span>
            </.link>

            <.link
              patch={archive_person_path(assigns)}
              data-role="archive-person-chip"
              data-active={to_string(@archive_person_id != nil)}
              class={[
                "inline-flex min-h-11 flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-3 text-xs transition sm:min-h-9",
                @archive_person_id && "border-primary bg-primary text-primary-content",
                is_nil(@archive_person_id) &&
                  "border-base-300 bg-base-100 text-base-content/60 hover:text-base-content"
              ]}
            >
              <span class="hero-user size-3.5 flex-shrink-0"></span>
              <span>{archive_person_label(assigns)}</span>
            </.link>

            <label class={[
              "inline-flex min-h-11 flex-shrink-0 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-3 text-xs transition sm:min-h-9",
              @archive_project_id && "border-primary bg-primary text-primary-content",
              is_nil(@archive_project_id) && "border-base-300 bg-base-100 text-base-content/60"
            ]}>
              <span class="hero-folder size-3.5 flex-shrink-0"></span>
              <select
                name="project_id"
                aria-label="Filter the archive by project"
                data-role="archive-project-filter"
                class="max-w-36 truncate bg-transparent text-xs outline-none"
              >
                <option value="" selected={is_nil(@archive_project_id)}>Any project</option>
                <option
                  :for={project <- assigns[:projects] || []}
                  value={project.id}
                  selected={project.id == @archive_project_id}
                >
                  {project.name}
                </option>
              </select>
            </label>
          </div>

          <div class="hidden flex-1 sm:block"></div>

          <span
            class="hidden flex-shrink-0 text-[10px] text-base-content/40 sm:inline"
            data-role="archive-sort-label"
          >
            {archive_sort_label(@archive_result)}
          </span>
        </form>

        <%!-- Results. The bottom padding clears the safe area AND the mobile
           bottom nav, matching the FAB offset below — nothing in the
           archive may come to rest under the nav row. --%>
        <div
          class="flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-3 py-3 pb-[calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom)+var(--bottom-nav-height,0px)+5rem)] sm:px-6 sm:py-5"
          data-role="archive-results"
        >
          <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-[840px] flex-col gap-5">
            <%!-- ── The two empty states ──────────────────────────────────
               Deliberately NOT one card with a swapped noun. "You have
               nothing archived" and "your search reached nothing" are
               different facts with different next actions, and a shared
               component would let a future edit collapse them back into the
               single ambiguous card this surface started with. They carry
               different `data-empty-state` values, different glyphs,
               different headings, and only one of them offers a control —
               there is nothing to clear when there is nothing to find.

               Their bottom padding clears the safe area and the mobile
               bottom nav on its own, because a centred card in a short
               archive is exactly the element that comes to rest under the
               nav row. --%>
            <div
              :if={archive_no_meetings?(@archive_result)}
              class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center py-16 pb-[calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom)+var(--bottom-nav-height,0px)+4rem)] text-center sm:pb-16"
              data-empty-state="archive-no-meetings"
            >
              <span class="hero-video-camera size-12 text-base-content/30"></span>
              <h2 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold" data-role="archive-no-meetings-heading">
                No meetings yet
              </h2>
            </div>

            <%!-- The matched partition is empty, but the archive is not — so
               this state renders ABOVE the "Also in this range, no match"
               section rather than instead of it. Showing the meetings that
               exist is precisely what distinguishes it from the state above,
               and hiding them would make the two indistinguishable to a
               reader no matter what the markup says. --%>
            <div
              :if={archive_no_matches?(@archive_result)}
              class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center py-16 pb-[calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom)+var(--bottom-nav-height,0px)+4rem)] text-center sm:pb-16"
              data-empty-state="archive-no-matches"
            >
              <span class="hero-magnifying-glass size-12 text-base-content/30"></span>
              <h2 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold" data-role="archive-no-matches-heading">
                No matches
              </h2>
              <.link
                patch={archive_clear_path(assigns)}
                data-action="archive-clear-filters"
                class="mt-4 inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-100 px-4 text-sm font-medium hover:border-primary hover:text-primary"
              >
                <span class="hero-arrow-uturn-left size-4 flex-shrink-0"></span>
                <span>Clear search and filters</span>
              </.link>
            </div>

            <%= for {section, index} <- Enum.with_index(archive_sections(@archive_result)) do %>
              <section data-archive-section={section.key}>
                <h2 class="mb-2 flex items-baseline gap-2 text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-base-content/40">
                  <%!-- The count lives in the header on desktop and HERE on a
                     phone, which has no room for it up top. --%>
                  <span :if={index == 0} class="sm:hidden" data-role="archive-count-mobile">
                    {archive_count_label(@archive_result, "by relevance")}
                  </span>
                  <span
                    :if={section.heading}
                    class={index == 0 && "hidden sm:inline"}
                    data-role={"archive-#{section.key}-heading"}
                  >
                    {section.heading}
                  </span>
                </h2>

                <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
                  <%= for entry <- section.rows,
                        artifacts = archive_artifacts(@archive_artifacts, entry) do %>
                    <article
                      class={[
                        "flex gap-3 rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-100 p-2.5",
                        section.dim? && "opacity-60"
                      ]}
                      data-role="archive-row"
                      data-meeting-id={entry.id}
                      data-meeting-status={entry.status}
                    >
                      <%!-- Date gutter — desktop only. The phone folds the
                         date into the subtitle instead. --%>
                      <div
                        class="hidden w-11 flex-shrink-0 border-r border-base-300 pr-2.5 text-center sm:block"
                        data-role="archive-row-date"
                      >
                        <div class="text-[9px] uppercase tracking-wide text-base-content/40">
                          {archive_row_month(entry, @archive_time_zone)}
                        </div>
                        <div class="text-lg font-semibold leading-tight text-base-content/70">
                          {archive_row_day(entry, @archive_time_zone)}
                        </div>
                      </div>

                      <div class="flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col">
                        <.link
                          navigate={~p"/meetings/#{entry.slug}"}
                          class="flex min-h-11 items-center text-sm font-semibold hover:text-primary sm:min-h-0"
                          data-role="archive-row-title"
                        >
                          <span class="truncate">{entry.name}</span>
                        </.link>

                        <span
                          class={[
                            "truncate text-xs sm:hidden",
                            entry.status == "cancelled" && "text-error",
                            entry.status != "cancelled" && "text-base-content/50"
                          ]}
                          data-role="archive-row-subtitle-mobile"
                        >
                          {archive_row_mobile_subtitle(entry, artifacts, @archive_time_zone)}
                        </span>
                        <span
                          class={[
                            "hidden truncate text-xs sm:block",
                            entry.status == "cancelled" && "text-error",
                            entry.status != "cancelled" && "text-base-content/50"
                          ]}
                          data-role="archive-row-subtitle"
                        >
                          {archive_row_subtitle(entry, artifacts)}
                        </span>

                        <%!-- Artifact chips WRAP at every width — they are the
                           row's content, not its chrome. --%>
                        <div class="mt-1.5 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5" data-role="archive-row-chips">
                          <span
                            :for={chip <- artifacts.chips}
                            class={[
                              "inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-1.5 rounded border px-2 text-[10px] sm:min-h-0 sm:py-0.5",
                              archive_chip_class(chip.type)
                            ]}
                            data-role="archive-chip"
                            data-chip-type={chip.type}
                          >
                            <span class={[archive_chip_icon(chip.type), "size-3 flex-shrink-0"]}>
                            </span>
                            <span
                              class={archive_chip_short_label(chip) && "hidden sm:inline"}
                              data-role="archive-chip-label"
                            >
                              {chip.label}
                            </span>
                            <span
                              :if={archive_chip_short_label(chip)}
                              class="sm:hidden"
                              data-role="archive-chip-label-short"
                            >
                              {archive_chip_short_label(chip)}
                            </span>
                          </span>

                          <span
                            :if={artifacts.chips == []}
                            class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded border border-base-300 bg-base-200 px-2 font-mono text-[10px] text-base-content/40 sm:min-h-0 sm:py-0.5"
                            data-role="archive-no-artifacts"
                          >
                            no artifacts
                          </span>
                        </div>

                        <%!-- The phone drops the stack BELOW the chips; the
                           desktop column keeps it to the right. --%>
                        <div
                          class="mt-2 flex items-center sm:hidden"
                          data-role="archive-attendees-mobile"
                          data-attendee-count={artifacts.attendee_count}
                        >
                          <span
                            :for={_ring <- archive_attendee_rings(artifacts)}
                            class="-ml-1.5 flex size-5 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-base-300 text-base-content/60 ring-2 ring-base-100 first:ml-0"
                          >
                            <span class="hero-user size-3"></span>
                          </span>
                          <span
                            :if={archive_attendee_overflow(artifacts) > 0}
                            class="ml-1.5 text-[10px] text-base-content/40"
                          >
                            +{archive_attendee_overflow(artifacts)}
                          </span>
                        </div>
                      </div>

                      <div
                        class="hidden flex-shrink-0 items-start pl-1.5 sm:flex"
                        data-role="archive-attendees"
                        data-attendee-count={artifacts.attendee_count}
                      >
                        <span
                          :for={_ring <- archive_attendee_rings(artifacts)}
                          class="-ml-1.5 flex size-5 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-base-300 text-base-content/60 ring-2 ring-base-100 first:ml-0"
                        >
                          <span class="hero-user size-3"></span>
                        </span>
                        <span
                          :if={archive_attendee_overflow(artifacts) > 0}
                          class="ml-1.5 text-[10px] text-base-content/40"
                        >
                          +{archive_attendee_overflow(artifacts)}
                        </span>
                      </div>
                    </article>
                  <% end %>
                </div>
              </section>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%!-- ── Calendar pane (task 019fb954-8ead) ──────────────────────────
         The region element and its `data-region` attribute are the
         archive sibling's pin and are reproduced verbatim; only its
         CHILDREN belong to this task.

         It has moved OUT of the else-arm of `if assigns[:meetings] in
         [nil, []]` and now renders whenever the calendar pane is
         showing. A calendar draws its chrome regardless of how many
         meetings it has: an empty week grid IS the answer to "what is
         on this week?", where a centred "no meetings yet" card is an
         answer to a question nobody asked of a calendar. The design
         frame draws empty day columns for exactly this reason.

         The `data-empty-state="meetings-index"` card and the grouped
         section/row list that stage 2 kept here as the below-`lg`
         surface are now GONE: the week strip and day view below are
         what stage 2 was holding the place for. Leaving them would
         draw both surfaces on a phone, one under the other. --%>
      <%!-- Stage 4 wraps the calendar region and the detail panel in one
         ROW so the panel can be frame 1's right rail. The region element
         below keeps its own `:if` even though this wrapper now carries the
         same guard: it and its `data-region` are the archive sibling's
         pin, reproduced byte-identically so a rollup merge has nothing to
         resolve on that line. The redundancy is the cheaper of the two. --%>
      <div :if={@meetings_view == :calendar} class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
        <div
          :if={@meetings_view == :calendar}
          class="flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-3 py-3 sm:px-6 sm:py-5"
          data-region="meetings-list"
        >
          <%!-- ── The viewer's whole scoped index is empty (019f9cb7 stage 5) ─
           Deliberately NOT the centred hero the archive's two empty states
           use, and not centred at all. This route is the redirect target for
           a `/meetings/:slug` the viewer may not see, so this block renders
           UNDER a "Meeting not found." flash more often than not, and two
           centred messages stacked read as a broken app rather than as an
           ordinary empty list. Left-aligned body text in the content column
           is subordinate to the flash, which is the correct relationship:
           the flash is the event, this is the resting state.

           It sits ABOVE the week nav and does not replace the grid. Zero
           meetings in total is a different fact from an empty week, and the
           calendar still has to be navigable — a viewer with no meetings can
           still page weeks and start one.

           No exclamation mark and no illustration: this is a normal state,
           not an apology or an error. --%>
          <div
            :if={@index_empty?}
            class="mb-4 max-w-prose"
            data-empty-state="meetings-index"
          >
            <h2 class="text-base font-semibold" data-role="meetings-index-empty-heading">
              No meetings yet.
            </h2>
            <%!-- Branching, not one string, because the two classes of viewer
             are empty for different reasons and each explanation is FALSE
             for the other. See `:viewer_external?` in `meeting_live.ex`. --%>
            <p
              :if={@viewer_external?}
              class="mt-1 text-sm text-base-content/60"
              data-role="meetings-index-empty-note"
              data-viewer="external"
            >
              You'll see a meeting here once you've been invited.
            </p>
            <p
              :if={!@viewer_external?}
              class="mt-1 text-sm text-base-content/60"
              data-role="meetings-index-empty-note"
              data-viewer="internal"
            >
              Meetings for your organisation will appear here.
            </p>
          </div>

          <%!-- Week nav. Renders at every width — paging the week and
           naming the zone are not desktop concerns, and stage 3's
           mobile strip sits under this rather than replacing it. --%>
          <div class="mb-3 flex items-center gap-1" data-role="calendar-week-nav">
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="calendar_prev_week"
              aria-label="Previous week"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm btn-square min-h-11"
            >
              <span class="hero-chevron-left size-4"></span>
            </button>

            <span class="px-1 text-sm font-semibold" data-role="calendar-week-label">
              {calendar_week_label(@calendar_anchor_date)}
            </span>

            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="calendar_next_week"
              aria-label="Next week"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm btn-square min-h-11"
            >
              <span class="hero-chevron-right size-4"></span>
            </button>

            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="calendar_today"
              class="btn btn-outline btn-sm ml-1 min-h-11"
            >
              Today
            </button>

            <div class="flex-1"></div>

            <%!-- Only when the zone is KNOWN. On the dead render there is
             no resolved zone, and printing `UTC` there would be a
             confident wrong answer — a calendar silently drawn in
             server time is wrong in a way nobody notices until they
             miss a call. --%>
            <span
              :if={@calendar_time_zone}
              class="truncate font-mono text-[10px] text-base-content/40"
              data-role="calendar-timezone"
              data-zone-assumed={@calendar_zone_assumed? && "true"}
            >
              {@calendar_time_zone}
            </span>

            <%!-- The qualifier is a SIBLING of the chip, not a child. An
             assumed zone must never read as a resolved one, but the
             resolved chip's text is pinned character-for-character by a
             test, and text inside the chip would change it for every
             viewer. So the attribute rides the chip and the words ride
             beside it. --%>
            <span
              :if={@calendar_zone_assumed?}
              class="ml-1 whitespace-nowrap text-[10px] font-medium text-warning"
              data-role="calendar-timezone-assumed-note"
            >
              assumed, not detected
            </span>
          </div>

          <%!-- ── The browser reported no usable zone ───────────────────────
           Modelled on the archive's PAIR of empty states (~1751 above),
           and for the archive's stated reason: this is a different fact
           from "the week is empty", with a different next action, and one
           card with a swapped noun would let a future edit collapse them
           back together. So this block is separate, carries its own
           `data-empty-state`, its own glyph, its own heading, and the
           only control on either state — a genuinely empty week has
           nothing to offer and renders no element here at all.

           It sits between the week nav and BOTH halves of the calendar so
           it appears at every width, and it is connected-only by
           construction: `:calendar_zone_unresolved?` is false on the dead
           render, which therefore stays byte-identical.

           Bottom padding mirrors the archive cards. `.bottom-nav` is
           `position: sticky` and takes flow space, so this clears the
           FAB, not the nav. --%>
          <div
            :if={@calendar_zone_unresolved?}
            class="mb-3 flex flex-col items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-warning/40 bg-warning/5 px-4 py-6 pb-[calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom)+var(--bottom-nav-height,0px)+4rem)] text-center sm:pb-16"
            data-empty-state="calendar-no-timezone"
          >
            <span class="hero-globe-alt size-12 text-warning/60"></span>
            <h2 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold" data-role="calendar-no-timezone-heading">
              Your browser did not report a usable time zone
            </h2>
            <p class="mt-1 max-w-md text-sm text-base-content/60">
              Meetings cannot be placed on a calendar without one, so none are drawn below. This
              is not a claim that your week is empty.
            </p>

            <%!-- Client-controlled data. Interpolated, never `raw/1`. --%>
            <p
              :if={@calendar_time_zone_raw}
              class="mt-2 break-all font-mono text-[11px] text-base-content/50"
              data-role="calendar-no-timezone-raw"
            >
              {@calendar_time_zone_raw}
            </p>

            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="calendar_assume_utc"
              data-action="calendar-assume-utc"
              class="btn btn-outline btn-sm mt-4 min-h-11"
            >
              <span class="hero-clock size-4 flex-shrink-0"></span>
              <span>Show this week in UTC</span>
            </button>
          </div>

          <%!-- ── Desktop week grid (`lg` and up) ──────────────────────────
           `hidden lg:grid` is one decision in two classes and both are
           load-bearing: `grid` alone would draw seven ~45px columns on
           a phone, `hidden` alone would draw nothing anywhere. The
           gutter is 3.25rem (52px) and each hour row is the same, so
           the seven day columns inherit their height from the gutter
           and no pixel arithmetic reaches the day cells. --%>
          <div
            data-role="calendar-week-grid"
            class="hidden overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-100 lg:grid"
            style="grid-template-columns: 3.25rem repeat(7, minmax(0, 1fr))"
          >
            <%!-- Header row: the gutter's corner, then seven day headings. --%>
            <div class="border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200/40"></div>
            <div
              :for={day <- calendar_week_days(@calendar_anchor_date, @calendar_time_zone)}
              class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1 border-b border-l border-base-300 bg-base-200/40 py-2"
              data-role="calendar-week-day-heading"
              data-date={Date.to_iso8601(day.date)}
            >
              <span class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-base-content/50">
                {calendar_weekday_abbrev(day.date)}
              </span>
              <span
                class={[
                  "size-6 text-center text-sm font-semibold leading-6",
                  day.today? && "rounded-full bg-primary text-primary-content"
                ]}
                data-today={day.today? && "true"}
              >
                {day.date.day}
              </span>
            </div>

            <%!-- Body row: the hour gutter, then seven day columns. --%>
            <div class="flex flex-col bg-base-200/20">
              <div
                :for={hour <- calendar_hours(@calendar_hour_window)}
                class="pr-1.5 pt-0.5 text-right font-mono text-[10px] leading-none text-base-content/40"
                style={calendar_hour_row_style(:week)}
              >
                {calendar_hour_label(hour)}
              </div>
            </div>

            <div
              :for={day <- calendar_week_days(@calendar_anchor_date, @calendar_time_zone)}
              class="relative border-l border-base-300"
              style={calendar_hour_rules_style(:week)}
              data-role="calendar-day-column"
              data-date={Date.to_iso8601(day.date)}
            >
              <%!-- The now-line lives INSIDE today's column, never spanning
               the grid: "now" is a fact about one day, and a rule drawn
               across all seven says it is 2:14 PM on Sunday too. It is
               absent entirely when the zone is unresolved or the current
               hour falls outside the drawn window.

               `@calendar_now` is passed IN rather than read inside the
               helper. That assign is what makes the line move: LiveView
               re-evaluates this expression when one of the assigns it
               names changes, and only then, so a helper reading the
               clock on its own draws whatever time the last unrelated
               re-render happened at. --%>
              <div
                :for={
                  offset <-
                    calendar_now_line(
                      day.today?,
                      @calendar_time_zone,
                      @calendar_hour_window,
                      @calendar_now
                    )
                }
                class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 z-10 h-[1.5px] bg-error"
                style={"top:#{offset}%"}
                data-role="calendar-now-line"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <span class="absolute -left-px -top-[3px] size-2 rounded-full bg-error"></span>
              </div>

              <%!-- The block carries NO pixel minimum height. It used to,
               and that is the defect the sponsor caught: a 44px floor in a
               52px hour row makes a half-hour block 18px taller than its
               own slot, so the next block starts inside it and the two
               labels overprint. The box is now exactly its share of the
               window, `CalendarWindow`'s half-hour floor keeps the
               smallest one from being a hairline, and blocks that
               genuinely coincide are separated sideways by lanes rather
               than stacked. --%>
              <button
                :for={
                  {meeting, placement} <-
                    calendar_day_events(
                      @calendar_week_meetings,
                      day.date,
                      @calendar_time_zone,
                      @calendar_hour_window,
                      :week
                    )
                }
                type="button"
                phx-click="select_meeting"
                phx-value-id={meeting.id}
                class={
                  [
                    "absolute flex overflow-hidden rounded-md border-l-[3px] px-1.5 py-1 text-left",
                    # A half-hour block is 26px here, which two lines do not
                    # fit in. Dropping to one line is the honest answer —
                    # growing the box instead is what collided.
                    placement.compact? && "flex-row items-center gap-1.5",
                    !placement.compact? && "flex-col items-start",
                    meeting.status == "live" && "border-success bg-success/15 hover:bg-success/25",
                    meeting.status != "live" && "border-primary bg-primary/15 hover:bg-primary/25",
                    @selected_meeting_id == meeting.id && "ring-2 ring-primary"
                  ]
                }
                style={calendar_block_style(placement, :week)}
                data-role="calendar-event"
                data-meeting-id={meeting.id}
                data-meeting-status={meeting.status}
                data-lane={placement.lane}
                data-lanes={placement.lanes}
                data-compact={placement.compact? && "true"}
                data-live={meeting.status == "live" && "true"}
                data-selected={@selected_meeting_id == meeting.id && "true"}
              >
                <%!-- On one line the two labels compete for the same
                 width, and in a three-lane column there is not enough for
                 both. The NAME wins: where a block sits already says when
                 it is, so a block showing only `14:15` identifies nothing,
                 while `Bud…` at quarter past still does. `flex-auto` (not
                 `flex-1`) is what makes that real — a zero basis cannot
                 claim space back off a shrinking sibling. --%>
                <span class={[
                  "flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1",
                  placement.compact? && "flex-auto",
                  !placement.compact? && "w-full"
                ]}>
                  <%!-- A shape, not a glyph: "live" is carried by motion
                   and by the accent token, so it survives both a
                   missing icon font and a monochrome reading. --%>
                  <span
                    :if={meeting.status == "live"}
                    class="size-1.5 flex-shrink-0 rounded-full bg-success animate-pulse"
                    data-role="calendar-event-live-dot"
                  >
                  </span>
                  <span class="truncate text-[11px] font-medium leading-tight text-base-content">
                    {meeting.name}
                  </span>
                </span>
                <span class={[
                  "truncate text-[10px] leading-tight text-base-content/60",
                  placement.compact? && "min-w-0 shrink-[10]"
                ]}>
                  {calendar_event_time(meeting, @calendar_time_zone)}
                </span>
              </button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <%!-- ── Below `lg`: week strip over a single-column day view ─────
           A DIFFERENT INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE, not a narrower grid.
           Seven columns at 375px leaves ~45px per day — titles are
           unreadable and blocks untappable — so the phone splits the
           two jobs the grid does at once: the strip keeps the WEEK
           legible for navigation, one column carries the DETAIL of the
           selected day.

           Every one of the three elements below carries its own
           `lg:hidden` rather than sitting inside one hidden wrapper.
           That is deliberate: a wrapper would make each element's own
           breakpoint class decorative, and a test pinning a class that
           does nothing is worse than no test. --%>
          <div
            data-role="calendar-week-strip"
            class="grid grid-cols-7 overflow-hidden rounded-t-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-200 lg:hidden"
          >
            <button
              :for={day <- calendar_week_days(@calendar_anchor_date, @calendar_time_zone)}
              type="button"
              phx-click="select_calendar_day"
              phx-value-date={Date.to_iso8601(day.date)}
              aria-current={Date.compare(day.date, @calendar_selected_date) == :eq && "date"}
              class="flex min-h-11 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-0.5 px-0 py-1.5 transition hover:bg-base-300/60"
              data-role="calendar-strip-day"
              data-date={Date.to_iso8601(day.date)}
              data-selected={Date.compare(day.date, @calendar_selected_date) == :eq && "true"}
              data-today={day.today? && "true"}
            >
              <%!-- Today stays identifiable even when another day is
               selected. The frames only ever draw a day that is both,
               so they do not answer this; without the tell, selecting
               Friday leaves a phone with nothing on screen that says
               which day it actually is. Carried by the accent token on
               the letter, NOT by a second circle, so the filled circle
               keeps meaning exactly one thing: selection. --%>
              <span class={[
                "text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase leading-none tracking-widest",
                day.today? && "text-primary",
                !day.today? && "text-base-content/50"
              ]}>
                {calendar_weekday_initial(day.date)}
              </span>
              <span
                class={[
                  "size-6 text-center text-sm font-semibold leading-6",
                  Date.compare(day.date, @calendar_selected_date) == :eq &&
                    "rounded-full bg-primary text-primary-content"
                ]}
                data-role="calendar-strip-day-number"
              >
                {day.date.day}
              </span>
            </button>
          </div>

          <%!-- The mobile count lives HERE, not in the page header. Both
           mobile frames strip the header back to icon + `Meetings` +
           the segmented control and put `Wednesday, Aug 5 · 2
           meetings` under the strip instead, where it describes the
           day actually being drawn rather than a week-wide total the
           single column cannot show. --%>
          <div
            class="flex items-baseline gap-1.5 border-x border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200/40 px-3 py-2 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-base-content/50 lg:hidden"
            data-role="calendar-day-heading"
            data-date={Date.to_iso8601(@calendar_selected_date)}
          >
            <span>{calendar_day_label(@calendar_selected_date)}</span>
            <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <%!-- With no zone the week was never queried, so the count is
             zero for a reason that has nothing to do with the day. Running
             it through `calendar_day_count_label/1` would print "No
             meetings" — the single most misleading string on this surface,
             and a confident false statement about the viewer's day. An
             em-dash says the count is unknown, which is the truth. --%>
            <span
              :if={@calendar_zone_unresolved?}
              data-role="calendar-day-count"
              data-count-state="unknown"
            >
              —
            </span>
            <%!-- The resolved arm carries NO `data-count-state`. Adding one
             would have changed the dead render's bytes, which stage 1
             pinned; absence is the second value. --%>
            <span :if={!@calendar_zone_unresolved?} data-role="calendar-day-count">
              {calendar_day_count_label(
                calendar_day_meeting_count(
                  @calendar_week_meetings,
                  @calendar_selected_date,
                  @calendar_time_zone,
                  @calendar_hour_window
                )
              )}
            </span>
          </div>

          <%!-- One day, one column, its own 44px gutter. The hour rows
           are 5.5rem here against the desktop grid's 3.25rem, and that
           costs nothing: `CalendarWindow.place/4` answers in
           PERCENTAGES of the drawn window, so both halves place the
           same meeting correctly at different row heights.

           5.5rem is DERIVED, not picked. The shortest block the placement
           maths will draw is half an hour, and half of 5.5rem is exactly
           the 44px touch target — so on a phone the smallest block is
           tappable because of its geometry, and needs no CSS minimum
           reaching down into the block below it. A shorter row would put
           the two constraints back in conflict, which is what the
           geometric test on this file pins.

           The bottom margin is FAB clearance, not nav clearance —
           `.bottom-nav` is `position: sticky` inside the shell's flex
           column (app.css:2226), so it takes flow space and the scroll
           region already ends above it. The FAB is the thing that
           overlays: it is fixed 1.5rem above the nav and is ~3rem
           tall, so without this the last hour sits under it. --%>
          <div
            data-role="calendar-day-view"
            class="mb-20 grid overflow-hidden rounded-b-lg border-x border-b border-base-300 bg-base-100 lg:hidden"
            style="grid-template-columns: 2.75rem 1fr"
          >
            <div class="flex flex-col bg-base-200/20">
              <div
                :for={hour <- calendar_hours(@calendar_hour_window)}
                class="pr-1.5 pt-0.5 text-right font-mono text-[10px] leading-none text-base-content/40"
                style={calendar_hour_row_style(:day)}
              >
                {calendar_hour_label(hour)}
              </div>
            </div>

            <div
              class="relative border-l border-base-300"
              style={calendar_hour_rules_style(:day)}
              data-role="calendar-day-body"
              data-date={Date.to_iso8601(@calendar_selected_date)}
            >
              <%!-- Same two conditions as the desktop column, asked of
               the SELECTED day rather than of each of seven: the line
               is a fact about today, so it is absent whenever the day
               on screen is not today or the zone is unresolved. --%>
              <div
                :for={
                  offset <-
                    calendar_selected_now_line(
                      @calendar_selected_date,
                      @calendar_time_zone,
                      @calendar_hour_window,
                      @calendar_now
                    )
                }
                class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 z-10 h-[1.5px] bg-error"
                style={"top:#{offset}%"}
                data-role="calendar-now-line"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <span class="absolute -left-px -top-[3px] size-2 rounded-full bg-error"></span>
              </div>

              <%!-- The SAME contract as the desktop block — same
               `data-role`, same id/status/live attributes, same
               percentage geometry, same lanes, same event — so one
               selector reads both halves and neither half can drift from
               the other. What differs is only what a thumb needs: more
               vertical padding, and taller hour rows, which is where the
               44px floor comes from now. There is no `min-h-` here for
               the same reason there is none on the desktop block: a pixel
               minimum inside a proportional column steals the next
               block's space and the labels collide.

               Lanes cannot arise from a narrower screen — this half has
               one day, not seven — but they arise from the same
               double-booking the week grid draws, so the phone splits the
               column too rather than stacking two meetings' text. --%>
              <button
                :for={
                  {meeting, placement} <-
                    calendar_day_events(
                      @calendar_week_meetings,
                      @calendar_selected_date,
                      @calendar_time_zone,
                      @calendar_hour_window,
                      :day
                    )
                }
                type="button"
                phx-click="select_meeting"
                phx-value-id={meeting.id}
                class={[
                  "absolute flex flex-col items-start justify-center overflow-hidden rounded-md border-l-[3px] px-2 py-1.5 text-left",
                  meeting.status == "live" && "border-success bg-success/15",
                  meeting.status != "live" && "border-primary bg-primary/15",
                  @selected_meeting_id == meeting.id && "ring-2 ring-primary"
                ]}
                style={calendar_block_style(placement, :day)}
                data-role="calendar-event"
                data-meeting-id={meeting.id}
                data-meeting-status={meeting.status}
                data-lane={placement.lane}
                data-lanes={placement.lanes}
                data-live={meeting.status == "live" && "true"}
                data-selected={@selected_meeting_id == meeting.id && "true"}
              >
                <span class="flex w-full min-w-0 items-center gap-1.5">
                  <span
                    :if={meeting.status == "live"}
                    class="size-1.5 flex-shrink-0 rounded-full bg-success animate-pulse"
                    data-role="calendar-event-live-dot"
                  >
                  </span>
                  <span class="truncate text-xs font-medium leading-tight text-base-content">
                    {meeting.name}
                  </span>
                </span>
                <span class="truncate text-[11px] leading-tight text-base-content/60">
                  {calendar_event_time(meeting, @calendar_time_zone)}
                </span>
              </button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <%!-- ── Meeting detail panel — desktop right rail (frame 1) ───────
           `hidden lg:flex` is one decision in two classes: the mobile
           surface for the same selection is the sheet below, and drawing
           both at 375px would stack a 322px rail under the day view.

           GEOMETRY — the panel CLIPS, the body SCROLLS, the footer is a
           SIBLING of the body. Making the panel itself the scroll region
           is the shape the sponsor rejected on the create sheet: with
           enough content the footer scrolls away with it. `min-h-0` on
           the body is what makes this real — a flex child defaults to
           `min-height: auto` and will not shrink below its content, so
           without it the body never scrolls and the footer is pushed out
           of the panel anyway. The per-invitee rows the create+invite
           member's next stage puts in this body are exactly the content
           that grows without bound. --%>
        <aside
          :if={detail_meeting}
          class="hidden w-[20.125rem] flex-none flex-col overflow-hidden border-l border-base-300 bg-base-100 lg:flex"
          data-role="meeting-detail-panel"
          aria-label="Meeting detail"
        >
          <div class="flex flex-none items-center gap-2 border-b border-base-300 px-4 py-3">
            <h2 class="min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-sm font-semibold">Meeting detail</h2>
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="close_meeting_detail"
              aria-label="Close meeting detail"
              title="Close"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm btn-square min-h-11"
              data-role="meeting-detail-panel-close"
            >
              <span class="hero-x-mark size-4"></span>
            </button>
          </div>

          <div
            class="flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col overflow-y-auto px-4 py-4"
            data-role="meeting-detail-panel-body"
          >
            <.meeting_detail_body
              meeting={detail_meeting}
              time_zone={@calendar_time_zone}
              invite_entries={detail_invites}
              id_prefix="meeting-detail-panel-invite"
              invite_open={@meeting_invite_open}
              invite_query={@meeting_invite_query}
              invite_candidates={@meeting_invite_candidates}
              invite_email_option={@meeting_invite_email}
            />
          </div>

          <div
            class="flex-none border-t border-base-300 px-4 py-3"
            data-role="meeting-detail-panel-footer"
          >
            <.meeting_detail_actions meeting={detail_meeting} />
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <%!-- ── Meeting detail sheet — below `lg` (mobile frame 2) ──────────
         The same selection, the same two shared components, a different
         presentation: a bottom sheet over the day view rather than a rail
         beside it. Shape is the create sheet's — `bg-black/40` backdrop
         that click-dismisses, `items-end` so it rises from the bottom
         edge a thumb starts at — plus the frames' drag handle.

         Out of flow, so `pb-[env(safe-area-inset-bottom)]` is the right
         clearance and `--bottom-nav-height` is NOT: the in-flow bottom
         nav reservation does not apply to an overlay that paints over it.

         Geometry is the panel's, for the same reason — see the panel's
         comment above. Here the cap is explicit (`max-h-[75vh]`) because
         a fixed overlay has no parent to inherit a height from. --%>
      <div
        :if={detail_meeting}
        class="lg:hidden fixed inset-0 z-50 flex items-end justify-center"
        data-role="meeting-detail-sheet"
        role="dialog"
        aria-modal="true"
        aria-labelledby="meeting-detail-sheet-title"
      >
        <div
          class="absolute inset-0 bg-black/40"
          phx-click="close_meeting_detail"
          aria-hidden="true"
          data-role="meeting-detail-sheet-backdrop"
        >
        </div>

        <div class="relative flex max-h-[75vh] w-full max-w-lg flex-col overflow-hidden rounded-t-2xl bg-base-100 pb-[env(safe-area-inset-bottom)] shadow-2xl">
          <div class="flex flex-none justify-center pt-2">
            <span
              class="h-1 w-8 rounded-full bg-base-300"
              data-role="meeting-detail-sheet-handle"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="flex flex-none items-center gap-2 border-b border-base-300 px-4 py-3">
            <h2
              id="meeting-detail-sheet-title"
              class="min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-sm font-semibold"
            >
              Meeting detail
            </h2>
            <button
              type="button"
              phx-click="close_meeting_detail"
              aria-label="Close meeting detail"
              title="Close"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm btn-square min-h-11"
              data-role="meeting-detail-sheet-close"
            >
              <span class="hero-x-mark size-4"></span>
            </button>
          </div>

          <div
            class="flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col overflow-y-auto px-4 py-4"
            data-role="meeting-detail-sheet-body"
          >
            <.meeting_detail_body
              meeting={detail_meeting}
              time_zone={@calendar_time_zone}
              invite_entries={detail_invites}
              id_prefix="meeting-detail-sheet-invite"
              invite_open={@meeting_invite_open}
              invite_query={@meeting_invite_query}
              invite_candidates={@meeting_invite_candidates}
              invite_email_option={@meeting_invite_email}
            />
          </div>

          <div
            class="flex-none border-t border-base-300 px-4 py-3"
            data-role="meeting-detail-sheet-footer"
          >
            <.meeting_detail_actions meeting={detail_meeting} />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%!-- ── Start-meeting FAB ───────────────────────────────────────────
         Mobile-first reachability fix: anchored bottom-right with a
         safe-area-aware bottom offset that ALSO clears the mobile
         bottom-nav (`--bottom-nav-height`, 56px on phones, 0 on
         desktop) so the FAB never sits behind the nav row. Mirrors
         the tasks_live FAB pattern (apps/platform/lib/platform_web/
         live/tasks_live.html.heex:2391). Only renders on `:index`
         (this branch is inside the `<%= if @meeting do … else …%>`
         else arm).

         `lg:hidden` (stage 5) makes this the MOBILE create path only; at
         `lg` and above the header's `new-meeting-button` takes over and
         pushes the same event. The bottom offset stays because a FAB is
         `position: fixed` — out of flow — which is exactly the case where
         reserving the in-flow nav height is correct.

         Stage 4 had carried an `lg:`-scoped horizontal offset — the panel's
         width plus a gutter — applied while the detail panel was open, so
         the FAB would not paint over the panel's action row. `lg:hidden`
         DELETES that state: the panel is `hidden lg:flex`, so it exists
         only at `lg` and above, which is precisely where the FAB no longer
         renders. The offset was therefore unreachable in every state and is
         gone rather than left behind as a guard that cannot fire. Below
         `lg` nothing overlaps either — the detail SHEET is a z-50 overlay
         above the FAB's z-30.

         Deliberately NOT naming that offset as a literal class string here:
         Tailwind scans this directory as plain text, so a class written in
         a COMMENT is still a build candidate and would emit the rule this
         change exists to retire.

         `min-h-11 min-w-11` is the 44px touch-target floor, and it is NOT
         redundant with `btn-circle`. Read out of the BUILT stylesheet
         rather than guessed from the class name: `.btn-circle` resolves to
         `width: var(--size); height: var(--size)`, `.btn` sets
         `--size: calc(var(--size-field, 0.25rem) * 10)`, and this app's
         theme sets `--size-field: 0.21875rem` — so the button was 35px,
         which is exactly what two reviewers measured. Nothing was
         overriding `btn-circle`; it was faithfully rendering a 35px token.

         The floor therefore has to be local. Raising `--size-field` would
         resize every button and input in the app to fix one button, and
         that token is a deliberate Fabrikam-spec value. And the floor is
         expressed as `min-*` rather than `size-11` on purpose: min-width
         and min-height beat width and height in the box model whatever
         order the two utility rules land in, so this does not depend on
         winning a cascade race with daisyUI.

         Safe here in a way a pixel floor was NOT safe on the calendar
         block: this button is `position: fixed`, so growing it consumes no
         sibling's slot. Its bottom offset is unchanged and still clears
         the nav — the offset is measured from the button's own bottom
         edge, so a taller button grows upward into empty space. --%>
      <button
        type="button"
        phx-click="open_start_meeting_sheet"
        aria-label="New meeting"
        title="New meeting"
        data-role="start-meeting-fab"
        class="btn btn-primary btn-circle fixed bottom-[calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom)+1.5rem+var(--bottom-nav-height,0px))] right-6 z-30 min-h-11 min-w-11 shadow-lg lg:hidden"
      >
        <span class="hero-video-camera size-6"></span>
      </button>

      <%!-- ── New-meeting create sheet ────────────────────────────────────
         Task 019fb955-1d7b stage 4. The sheet CHROME is unchanged from
         the stage-2 start-meeting sheet — full-bleed slide-up below
         `sm`, centred card above, `bg-black/40` backdrop that
         click-dismisses — because that is already the geometry the
         canvas draws. What changed is what the panel holds: a
         schedulable time, a required project, and a staged invite list.

         `data-role` values from the old sheet are KEPT even where the
         copy moved on (`start-meeting-*`); tests and sibling members key
         on them. New controls take `meeting-create-*`.

         Out of flow, so `pb-[env(safe-area-inset-bottom)]` is the right
         clearance here — the in-flow bottom-nav reservation does not
         apply to an overlay. --%>
      <div
        :if={@show_start_meeting_sheet}
        class="fixed inset-0 z-50 flex items-end sm:items-center justify-center"
        data-role="start-meeting-sheet"
        role="dialog"
        aria-modal="true"
        aria-labelledby="start-meeting-sheet-title"
      >
        <%!-- Backdrop (click to dismiss) --%>
        <div
          class="absolute inset-0 bg-black/40"
          phx-click="close_start_meeting_sheet"
          aria-hidden="true"
          data-role="start-meeting-sheet-backdrop"
        >
        </div>

        <%!-- Sheet panel. A flex COLUMN whose height is capped, not a single
             scroll region: the cap belongs to the panel and the scrolling
             belongs to the body alone, so the footer bar stays pinned no
             matter how many invites are staged. Scrolling the whole panel
             instead (`max-h-[75vh] overflow-y-auto` here) pushes the footer
             — and with it `start-meeting-submit` — off the bottom of the
             modal once the staged list is long enough, which is the defect
             the sponsor rejected stage 4 for.

             `overflow-hidden` keeps the body's scroll from painting over
             the rounded corners. --%>
        <div class="relative flex flex-col w-full max-w-lg rounded-t-2xl sm:rounded-2xl bg-base-100 shadow-2xl pb-[env(safe-area-inset-bottom)] max-h-[75vh] overflow-hidden">
          <%!-- Header. Below `sm` the primary action lives HERE (mobile
               frame 3): the footer bar is the first thing a soft keyboard
               covers, so the commit control has to be reachable above it. --%>
          <div class="flex-none flex items-center justify-between gap-2 border-b border-base-300 px-4 py-3">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 min-w-0">
              <span class="hero-video-camera size-5 flex-shrink-0 text-primary"></span>
              <h2 id="start-meeting-sheet-title" class="text-base font-semibold">
                New meeting
              </h2>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-1">
              <button
                type="submit"
                form="start-meeting-form"
                class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm px-3 font-semibold text-primary min-h-11 sm:hidden"
                data-role="meeting-create-submit-mobile"
              >
                Create
              </button>
              <button
                type="button"
                phx-click="close_start_meeting_sheet"
                class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square"
                aria-label="Close"
                data-role="start-meeting-sheet-close"
              >
                <span class="hero-x-mark size-4"></span>
              </button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <%!-- Form --%>
          <form
            id="start-meeting-form"
            phx-submit="start_meeting"
            phx-change="start_meeting_form_change"
            class="flex flex-1 flex-col min-h-0"
          >
            <%!-- Scrolling body. `min-h-0` is load-bearing: a flex child
                 defaults to `min-height: auto` and refuses to shrink below
                 its content, so without it this never scrolls and the
                 footer is pushed out of the panel again. --%>
            <div
              class="flex flex-1 flex-col gap-3 min-h-0 overflow-y-auto px-4 py-4"
              data-role="meeting-create-body"
            >
              <%!-- Inline error (server-side validation feedback) --%>
              <div
                :if={@start_meeting_error}
                class="rounded-md border border-error/40 bg-error/10 px-3 py-2 text-xs text-error"
                data-role="start-meeting-error"
              >
                {@start_meeting_error}
              </div>

              <%!-- Name (required) --%>
              <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                <label
                  for="start-meeting-name"
                  class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-base-content/60"
                >
                  Name <span class="text-error">*</span>
                </label>
                <input
                  id="start-meeting-name"
                  type="text"
                  name="start_meeting[name]"
                  value={@start_meeting_form["name"]}
                  required
                  autocomplete="off"
                  placeholder="What's the meeting about?"
                  class="input input-bordered w-full text-sm font-semibold min-h-11"
                  data-role="start-meeting-name"
                />
              </div>

              <%!-- Date / Start / Duration. Three across from `sm` up; below
                 it, Date takes its own row and Start + Duration pair, so
                 no control is narrower than its own content at 375px.
                 Leaving Date and Start blank creates an AD-HOC meeting —
                 `create_meeting/1` takes the intended-time pair as
                 both-or-neither and both nil is a supported state. --%>
              <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-[1.4fr_1fr_1fr] gap-2">
                <div class="col-span-2 flex flex-col gap-1 sm:col-span-1">
                  <label
                    for="meeting-create-date"
                    class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-base-content/60"
                  >
                    Date
                  </label>
                  <input
                    id="meeting-create-date"
                    type="date"
                    name="start_meeting[date]"
                    value={@start_meeting_form["date"]}
                    class="input input-bordered w-full text-sm min-h-11"
                    data-role="meeting-create-date"
                  />
                </div>

                <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                  <label
                    for="meeting-create-start-time"
                    class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-base-content/60"
                  >
                    Start
                  </label>
                  <input
                    id="meeting-create-start-time"
                    type="time"
                    name="start_meeting[start_time]"
                    value={@start_meeting_form["start_time"]}
                    class="input input-bordered w-full text-sm min-h-11"
                    data-role="meeting-create-start-time"
                  />
                </div>

                <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                  <label
                    for="meeting-create-duration"
                    class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-base-content/60"
                  >
                    Duration
                  </label>
                  <select
                    id="meeting-create-duration"
                    name="start_meeting[duration_minutes]"
                    class="select select-bordered w-full text-sm min-h-11"
                    data-role="meeting-create-duration"
                  >
                    <option
                      :for={minutes <- PlatformWeb.MeetingLive.duration_options()}
                      value={minutes}
                      selected={@start_meeting_form["duration_minutes"] == to_string(minutes)}
                    >
                      {PlatformWeb.MeetingLive.duration_label(minutes)}
                    </option>
                  </select>
                </div>
              </div>

              <%!-- Project (required). The `required` attribute is a hint;
                 the server-side guard in `handle_event("start_meeting", …)`
                 is the gate, because `meetings.project_id` is NOT NULL and
                 the slug is unique per project. --%>
              <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                <label
                  for="start-meeting-project"
                  class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-base-content/60"
                >
                  Project <span class="text-error">*</span>
                </label>
                <select
                  id="start-meeting-project"
                  name="start_meeting[project_id]"
                  required
                  class="select select-bordered w-full text-sm min-h-11"
                  data-role="start-meeting-project"
                >
                  <option value="" disabled selected={@start_meeting_form["project_id"] == ""}>
                    Select a project…
                  </option>
                  <option
                    :for={project <- assigns[:projects] || []}
                    value={project.id}
                    selected={@start_meeting_form["project_id"] == project.id}
                  >
                    {project.name}
                  </option>
                </select>
              </div>

              <%!-- Invite. `:draft` mode — nothing is written until submit,
                 and the row `X` unstages rather than revoking. --%>
              <div class="flex flex-col gap-2" data-role="meeting-create-invite-section">
                <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-base-content/60">
                  Invite
                </span>

                <.invite_control
                  mode={:draft}
                  id="meeting-create-invite"
                  query={@meeting_create_invite_query}
                  candidates={@meeting_create_invite_candidates}
                  email_option={@meeting_create_invite_email}
                />

                <.invite_list
                  mode={:draft}
                  entries={PlatformWeb.MeetingLive.draft_invite_rows(@meeting_create_invites)}
                />
              </div>
            </div>

            <%!-- Footer actions. Hidden below `sm`, where the header's
                 `Create` carries the same submit. OUTSIDE the scrolling
                 body and `flex-none`, so a long staged-invite list scrolls
                 under it instead of pushing it off the panel. --%>
            <div
              class="hidden flex-none items-center justify-end gap-2 border-t border-base-300 px-4 py-3 min-h-11 sm:flex"
              data-role="meeting-create-footer"
            >
              <button
                type="button"
                phx-click="close_start_meeting_sheet"
                class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm min-h-11"
                data-role="start-meeting-cancel"
              >
                Cancel
              </button>
              <button
                type="submit"
                class="btn btn-primary btn-sm gap-1.5 min-h-11"
                data-role="start-meeting-submit"
              >
                <span class="hero-paper-airplane size-4"></span>
                <span>Create &amp; send invites</span>
              </button>
            </div>
          </form>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
<% end %>
